complicated URL is:"},"491":{"id":"/docs/faq-53","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#thin-driver","content":"Please refer to the Apache Avatica documentation for a full list of supported options in the Thin client JDBC URL,\nor see the Query Server documentation"},"492":{"id":"/docs/faq-54","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#map-reduce","content":"See the example here"},"493":{"id":"/docs/faq-55","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#csv","content":"CSV data can be bulk loaded with built in utility named psql. Typical upsert rates are 20K - 50K rows per second (depends on how wide are the rows)."},"494":{"id":"/docs/faq-56","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#csv","content":"Usage example:"},"495":{"id":"/docs/faq-57","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#csv","content":"Create table using psql:"},"496":{"id":"/docs/faq-58","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#csv","content":"Upsert CSV bulk data:"},"497":{"id":"/docs/faq-59","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"You can create both a Phoenix table or view through the CREATE TABLE/CREATE VIEW DDL statement on a pre-existing HBase table. In both cases, we'll leave the HBase metadata as-is. For CREATE TABLE, we'll create any metadata (table, column families) that doesn't already exist. We'll also add an empty key value for each row so that queries behave as expected (without requiring all columns to be projected during scans)."},"498":{"id":"/docs/faq-60","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"The other caveat is that the way the bytes were serialized must match the way the bytes are serialized by Phoenix. For VARCHAR, CHAR, and UNSIGNED_* types, we use the HBase Bytes methods. The CHAR type expects only single-byte characters and the UNSIGNED types expect values greater than or equal to zero. For signed types (TINYINT, SMALLINT, INTEGER and BIGINT), Phoenix will flip the first bit so that negative values will sort before positive values. Because HBase sorts row keys in lexicographical order and negative value's first bit is 1 while positive 0 so that negative value is 'greater than' positive value if we don't flip the first bit. So if you stored integers by HBase native API and want to access them by Phoenix, make sure that all your data types are UNSIGNED types."},"499":{"id":"/docs/faq-61","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"Our composite row keys are formed by simply concatenating the values together, with a zero byte character used as a separator after a variable length type."},"500":{"id":"/docs/faq-62","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"If you create an HBase table like this:"},"501":{"id":"/docs/faq-63","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"then you have an HBase table with a name of t1 and a column family with a name of f1. Remember, in HBase, you don't model the possible KeyValues or the structure of the row key. This is the information you specify in Phoenix above and beyond the table and column family."},"502":{"id":"/docs/faq-64","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"So in Phoenix, you'd create a view like this:"},"503":{"id":"/docs/faq-65","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"The pk column declares that your row key is a VARCHAR (i.e. a string) while the \"f1\".val column declares that your HBase table will contain KeyValues with a column family and column qualifier of \"f1\":VAL and that their value will be a VARCHAR."},"504":{"id":"/docs/faq-66","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"Note that you don't need the double quotes if you create your HBase table with all caps names (since this is how Phoenix normalizes strings, by upper casing them). For example, with:"},"505":{"id":"/docs/faq-67","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"you could create this Phoenix view:"},"506":{"id":"/docs/faq-68","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-i-map-phoenix-table-to-an-existing-hbase-table","content":"Or if you're creating new HBase tables, just let Phoenix do everything for you like this (No need to use the HBase shell at all.):"},"507":{"id":"/docs/faq-69","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Use Salting to increase read/write performanceSalting can significantly increase read/write performance by pre-splitting the data into multiple regions. Although Salting will yield better performance in most scenarios.Example:"},"508":{"id":"/docs/faq-70","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Note: Ideally for a 16 region server cluster with quad-core CPUs, choose salt buckets between 32-64 for optimal performance."},"509":{"id":"/docs/faq-71","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Pre-split tableSalting does automatic table splitting but in case you want to exactly control where table split occurs with out adding extra byte or change row key order then you can pre-split a table.Example:"},"510":{"id":"/docs/faq-72","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Use multiple column familiesColumn family contains related data in separate files. If you query use selected columns then it make sense to group those columns together in a column family to improve read performance.Example:Following create table DDL will create two column faimiles A and B."},"511":{"id":"/docs/faq-73","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Use compressionOn disk compression improves performance on large tablesExample:"},"512":{"id":"/docs/faq-74","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Create indexes\nSee How do I connect to secure HBase cluster?"},"513":{"id":"/docs/faq-75","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Optimize cluster parameters\nSee https://hbase.apache.org/docs/performance"},"514":{"id":"/docs/faq-76","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#are-there-any-tips-for-optimizing-phoenix","content":"Optimize Phoenix parameters\nSee Configuration"},"515":{"id":"/docs/faq-77","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-do-i-create-secondary-index-on-a-table","content":"Starting with Phoenix version 2.1, Phoenix supports index over mutable and immutable data. Note that Phoenix 2.0.x only supports Index over immutable data. Index write performance index with immutable table is slightly faster than mutable table however data in immutable table cannot be updated."},"516":{"id":"/docs/faq-78","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-do-i-create-secondary-index-on-a-table","content":"Example:"},"517":{"id":"/docs/faq-79","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-do-i-create-secondary-index-on-a-table","content":"Create tableImmutable table: create table test (mykey varchar primary key, col1 varchar, col2 varchar) IMMUTABLE_ROWS=true;Mutable table: create table test (mykey varchar primary key, col1 varchar, col2 varchar);"},"518":{"id":"/docs/faq-80","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-do-i-create-secondary-index-on-a-table","content":"Creating index on col2create index idx on test (col2)"},"519":{"id":"/docs/faq-81","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-do-i-create-secondary-index-on-a-table","content":"Creating index on col1 and a covered index on col2create index idx on test (col1) include (col2)Upsert rows in this test table and Phoenix query optimizer will choose correct index to use. You can see in explain plan if Phoenix is using the index table. You can also give a hint in Phoenix query to use a specific index."},"520":{"id":"/docs/faq-82","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-do-i-create-secondary-index-on-a-table","content":"See Secondary Indexing for further information"},"521":{"id":"/docs/faq-83","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-secondary-index-being-used","content":"The secondary index won't be used unless all columns used in the query are in it ( as indexed or covered columns). All columns making up the primary key of the data table will automatically be included in the index."},"522":{"id":"/docs/faq-84","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-secondary-index-being-used","content":"Example: DDL create table usertable (id varchar primary key, firstname varchar, lastname varchar); create index idx_name on usertable (firstname);"},"523":{"id":"/docs/faq-85","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-secondary-index-being-used","content":"Query: DDL select id, firstname, lastname from usertable where firstname = 'foo';"},"524":{"id":"/docs/faq-86","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-secondary-index-being-used","content":"Index would not be used in this case as lastname is not part of indexed or covered column. This can be verified by looking at the explain plan. To fix this create index that has either lastname part of index or covered column. Example: create idx_name on usertable (firstname) include (lastname);"},"525":{"id":"/docs/faq-87","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-secondary-index-being-used","content":"You can force Phoenix to use secondary for uncovered columns by specifying an index hint"},"526":{"id":"/docs/faq-88","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-fast-is-phoenix-why-is-it-so-fast","content":"Phoenix is fast. Full table scan of 100M rows usually completes in 20 seconds (narrow table on a medium sized cluster). This time come down to few milliseconds if query contains filter on key columns. For filters on non-key columns or non-leading key columns, you can add index on these columns which leads to performance equivalent to filtering on key column by making copy of table with indexed column(s) part of key."},"527":{"id":"/docs/faq-89","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-fast-is-phoenix-why-is-it-so-fast","content":"Why is Phoenix fast even when doing full scan:"},"528":{"id":"/docs/faq-90","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-fast-is-phoenix-why-is-it-so-fast","content":"Phoenix chunks up your query using the region boundaries and runs them in parallel on the client using a configurable number of threads."},"529":{"id":"/docs/faq-91","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-fast-is-phoenix-why-is-it-so-fast","content":"The aggregation will be done in a coprocessor on the server-side, collapsing the amount of data that gets returned back to the client rather than returning it all."},"530":{"id":"/docs/faq-92","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#how-do-i-connect-to-secure-hbase-cluster","content":"Specify the principal and corresponding keytab in the JDBC URL as show above.\nFor ancient Phoenix versions heck out the excellent post by Anil Gupta"},"531":{"id":"/docs/faq-93","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#what-hbase-and-hadoop-versions-are-supported","content":"Phoenix 4.x supports HBase 1.x running on Hadoop 2"},"532":{"id":"/docs/faq-94","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#what-hbase-and-hadoop-versions-are-supported","content":"Phoenix 5.x supports HBase 2.x running on Hadoop 3"},"533":{"id":"/docs/faq-95","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#what-hbase-and-hadoop-versions-are-supported","content":"See the release notes and BUILDING in recent releases for the exact versions supported,\nand on how to build Phoenix for specific HBase and Hadoop versions"},"534":{"id":"/docs/faq-96","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#can-phoenix-work-on-tables-with-arbitrary-timestamp-as-flexible-as-hbase-api","content":"By default, Phoenix lets HBase manage the timestamps and just shows you the latest values for everything. However, Phoenix also allows arbitrary timestamps to be supplied by the user. To do that you'd specify a CurrentSCN at connection time, like this:"},"535":{"id":"/docs/faq-97","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#can-phoenix-work-on-tables-with-arbitrary-timestamp-as-flexible-as-hbase-api","content":"The above is equivalent to doing this with the HBase API:"},"536":{"id":"/docs/faq-98","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#can-phoenix-work-on-tables-with-arbitrary-timestamp-as-flexible-as-hbase-api","content":"By specifying a CurrentSCN, you're telling Phoenix that you want everything for that connection to be done at that timestamp. Note that this applies to queries done on the connection as well - for example, a query over myTable above would not see the data it just upserted, since it only sees data that was created before its CurrentSCN property. This provides a way of doing snapshot, flashback, or point-in-time queries."},"537":{"id":"/docs/faq-99","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#can-phoenix-work-on-tables-with-arbitrary-timestamp-as-flexible-as-hbase-api","content":"Keep in mind that creating a new connection is not an expensive operation. The same underlying HConnection is used for all connections to the same cluster, so it's more or less like instantiating a few objects."},"538":{"id":"/docs/faq-100","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-query-doing-a-range-scan","content":"RANGE SCAN means that only a subset of the rows in your table will be scanned over. This occurs if you use one or more leading columns from your primary key constraint. Query that is not filtering on leading PK columns ex. select * from test where pk2='x' and pk3='y'; will result in full scan whereas the following query will result in range scan select * from test where pk1='x' and pk2='y';. Note that you can add a secondary index on your pk2 and pk3 columns and that would cause a range scan to be done for the first query (over the index table)."},"539":{"id":"/docs/faq-101","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-query-doing-a-range-scan","content":"DEGENERATE SCAN means that a query can't possibly return any rows. If we can determine that at compile time, then we don't bother to even run the scan."},"540":{"id":"/docs/faq-102","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-query-doing-a-range-scan","content":"FULL SCAN means that all rows of the table will be scanned over (potentially with a filter applied if you have a WHERE clause)"},"541":{"id":"/docs/faq-103","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-isnt-my-query-doing-a-range-scan","content":"SKIP SCAN means that either a subset or all rows in your table will be scanned over, however it will skip large groups of rows depending on the conditions in your filter. See this blog for more detail. We don't do a SKIP SCAN if you have no filter on the leading primary key columns, but you can force a SKIP SCAN by using the /*+ SKIP_SCAN */ hint. Under some conditions, namely when the cardinality of your leading primary key columns is low, it will be more efficient than a FULL SCAN."},"542":{"id":"/docs/faq-104","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#should-i-pool-phoenix-jdbc-connections","content":"No, it is not necessary to pool Phoenix JDBC Connections."},"543":{"id":"/docs/faq-105","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#should-i-pool-phoenix-jdbc-connections","content":"Phoenix's Connection objects are different from most other JDBC Connections due to the underlying HBase connection. The Phoenix Connection object is designed to be a thin object that is inexpensive to create. If Phoenix Connections are reused, it is possible that the underlying HBase connection is not always left in a healthy state by the previous user. It is better to create new Phoenix Connections to ensure that you avoid any potential issues."},"544":{"id":"/docs/faq-106","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#should-i-pool-phoenix-jdbc-connections","content":"Implementing pooling for Phoenix could be done simply by creating a delegate Connection that instantiates a new Phoenix connection when retrieved from the pool and then closes the connection when returning it to the pool (see PHOENIX-2388)."},"545":{"id":"/docs/faq-107","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-does-phoenix-add-an-emptydummy-keyvalue-when-doing-an-upsert","content":"The empty or dummy KeyValue (with a column qualifier of _0) is needed to ensure that a given column is available\nfor all rows."},"546":{"id":"/docs/faq-108","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-does-phoenix-add-an-emptydummy-keyvalue-when-doing-an-upsert","content":"As you may know, data is stored in HBase as KeyValues, meaning that\nthe full row key is stored for each column value. This also implies\nthat the row key is not stored at all unless there is at least one\ncolumn stored."},"547":{"id":"/docs/faq-109","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-does-phoenix-add-an-emptydummy-keyvalue-when-doing-an-upsert","content":"Now consider JDBC row which has an integer primary key, and several\ncolumns which are all null. In order to be able to store the primary\nkey, a KeyValue needs to be stored to show that the row is present at\nall. This column is represented by the empty column that you've\nnoticed. This allows doing a SELECT * FROM TABLE and receiving\nrecords for all rows, even those whose non-pk columns are null."},"548":{"id":"/docs/faq-110","page_id":"/docs/faq","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq#why-does-phoenix-add-an-emptydummy-keyvalue-when-doing-an-upsert","content":"The same issue comes up even if only one column is null for some (or\nall) records. A scan over Phoenix will include the empty column to\nensure that rows that only consist of the primary key (and have null\nfor all non-key columns) will be included in a scan result."},"549":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","type":"page","content":"Atomic Upsert","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert"},"550":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-0","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","content":"Use UPSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY for atomic row-level updates in Phoenix, with examples and limitations."},"551":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-1","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-examples","content":"Examples"},"552":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-2","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE_ONLY"},"553":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-3","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"Returning the affected row"},"554":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-4","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"Java JDBC thick-client API"},"555":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-5","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"Limitations"},"556":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-6","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","content":"To support atomic upsert, an optional ON DUPLICATE KEY clause, similar to the MySQL syntax, has been\nincorporated into the UPSERT VALUES command as of Phoenix 4.9. The general syntax is described\nhere. This feature provides a superset of the HBase Increment and\nCheckAndPut functionality to enable atomic upserts. On the server-side, when the commit\nis processed, the row being updated will be locked while the current column values are read and the\nON DUPLICATE KEY clause is executed. Given that the row must be locked and read when the ON DUPLICATE KEY\nclause is used, there will be a performance penalty (much like there is for an HBase Put versus a CheckAndPut)."},"557":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-7","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","content":"In the presence of the ON DUPLICATE KEY clause, if the row already exists, the VALUES specified will\nbe ignored and instead either:"},"558":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-8","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","content":"the row will not be updated if ON DUPLICATE KEY IGNORE is specified or"},"559":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-9","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","content":"the row will be updated (under lock) by executing the expressions following the ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE\nclause."},"560":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-10","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","content":"Multiple UPSERT statements for the same row in the same commit batch will be processed in the order of their\nexecution. Thus the same result will be produced when auto commit is on or off."},"561":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-11","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-examples","content":"For example, to atomically increment two counter columns, you would execute the following command:"},"562":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-12","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-examples","content":"To only update a column if it doesn't yet exist:"},"563":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-13","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-examples","content":"Note that arbitrarily complex expressions may be used in this new clause:"},"564":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-14","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"UPDATE_ONLY is a third variant of the ON DUPLICATE KEY clause, available from\nPhoenix 5.3.0 (PHOENIX-7648).\nUnlike UPDATE, it only runs the update-clause expressions when the row already\nexists — it never inserts. If the row is missing, the supplied VALUES are discarded\nand the statement is a no-op. Use it when an UPSERT should be able to update an\nexisting row but must not create a new one."},"565":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-15","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"If id = 'sku-42' already exists, its qty is decremented under the row lock.\nIf it doesn't, the supplied VALUES are discarded and nothing is written."},"566":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-16","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"The three forms compared:"},"567":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-17","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Clause"},"568":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-18","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Row missing"},"569":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-19","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Row present"},"570":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-20","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"ON DUPLICATE KEY IGNORE"},"571":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-21","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Insert from VALUES"},"572":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-22","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"No-op"},"573":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-23","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE ..."},"574":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-24","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Insert from VALUES"},"575":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-25","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Run the update"},"576":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-26","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE_ONLY ..."},"577":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-27","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"No-op (VALUES discarded)"},"578":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-28","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Run the update"},"579":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-29","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"A single-row UPSERT or DELETE can append RETURNING * to return the affected row\nas a JDBC ResultSet in the same round-trip, available from Phoenix 5.3.0\n(PHOENIX-7651). The returned\nrow reflects the server-side state after the mutation — including any values\ncomputed by an ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE clause under the row lock."},"580":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-30","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"This collapses two common patterns into one round-trip:"},"581":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-31","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"Atomic read-modify-write counters — increment, then read the new value\nwithout a follow-up SELECT (and without risking a different writer slipping in\nbetween)."},"582":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-32","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"Tombstoning / queue consumers — atomically delete a row and read the payload\nyou just removed, useful for idempotent replay and \"claim the next task\" patterns."},"583":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-33","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"The statement must affect a single row when RETURNING * is used. Drivers iterate\nthe returned ResultSet exactly like the result of a regular SELECT."},"584":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-34","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"Java applications using the Phoenix thick JDBC client can drive the same\natomic-return semantics directly through typed methods on PhoenixStatement and\nPhoenixPreparedStatement, without putting RETURNING * in the SQL:"},"585":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-35","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"executeAtomicUpdateReturnRow — returns the row state after the mutation\n(or the unchanged row when the statement was a no-op). Equivalent to\nRETURNING * in SQL."},"586":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-36","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"executeAtomicUpdateReturnOldRow — returns the row state before the\nmutation, regardless of whether the mutation was applied. There is no SQL form\nfor this — only the typed API exposes the pre-image, which is the natural fit\nfor audit logs and compare-and-swap-style flows."},"587":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-37","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"Both return a Pair<Integer, ResultSet> where the integer is 1 if the row was\nmutated and 0 for a no-op, so callers know the outcome without having to inspect\ncell contents. Both require an auto-commit connection."},"588":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-38","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"The following limitations are enforced for the ON DUPLICATE KEY clause usage:"},"589":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-39","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"Primary key columns may not be updated, since this would essentially be creating a new row."},"590":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-40","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"Transactional tables may not use this clause as atomic upserts are already possible through\nexception handling when a conflict occurs."},"591":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-41","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"Immutable tables may not use this clause as by definition there should be no updates to\nexisting rows."},"592":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-42","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"The CURRENT_SCN property may not be set on connection when this clause is used as HBase\ndoes not handle atomicity unless the latest value is being updated."},"593":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-43","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"The same column should not be updated more than once in the same statement."},"594":{"id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert-44","page_id":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/atomic-upsert#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"No aggregation or references to sequences are allowed within the clause."},"595":{"id":"/docs/features/bson","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"page","content":"Document Data: BSON","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson"},"596":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-0","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson","content":"Store, query, filter, and atomically update Binary JSON (BSON) documents inside a Phoenix table — without round-tripping the document to the client."},"597":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-1","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"When to reach for BSON"},"598":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-2","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-defining","content":"Defining a BSON column"},"599":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-3","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-paths","content":"Field paths"},"600":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-4","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-value","content":"Reading fields: BSON_VALUE"},"601":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-5","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"Filtering rows: BSON_CONDITION_EXPRESSION"},"602":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-6","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"Atomically updating fields: BSON_UPDATE_EXPRESSION"},"603":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-7","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-indexing","content":"Indexing individual fields"},"604":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-8","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-end-to-end","content":"End-to-end example"},"605":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-9","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-limitations","content":"Limitations"},"606":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-10","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson","content":"BSON is a native column type for storing schemaless document data alongside your\nrelational columns. Documents are stored in Binary JSON — a\ncompact, length-prefixed format that is cheap to parse server-side and supports the\nfull set of value types (strings, numbers, dates, binary, booleans, nulls, arrays,\nnested objects). Introduced in Phoenix 5.3.0\n(PHOENIX-7330)."},"607":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-11","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson","content":"The point of the type is not just to hold documents — it's that Phoenix can read,\nfilter, and mutate individual fields on the server without the client ever\ndeserializing the whole document."},"608":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-12","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Use case"},"609":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-13","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Recommended type"},"610":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-14","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Schema is well-known and stable"},"611":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-15","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Regular relational columns"},"612":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-16","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"A few optional/sparse fields on otherwise relational rows"},"613":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-17","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Dynamic Columns"},"614":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-18","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Variable-shape documents per row, server-side reads/filters/updates needed"},"615":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-19","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"BSON"},"616":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-20","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Variable-shape documents but the server never inspects them"},"617":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-21","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"VARCHAR (raw JSON) or VARBINARY"},"618":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-22","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Common cases for BSON:"},"619":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-23","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"DynamoDB-style application objects backed by Phoenix."},"620":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-24","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Customer / configuration / preferences documents that vary per row."},"621":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-25","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Event payloads where downstream queries need to filter or project specific fields."},"622":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-26","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-when","content":"Counters and per-field updates that must be atomic without rewriting the whole row."},"623":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-27","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-defining","content":"You may pass a JSON string literal as shown — Phoenix parses and converts it to BSON on\nwrite — or bind a pre-built BSON document via JDBC."},"624":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-28","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-defining","content":"A BSON column may also serve as a primary-key column. In a composite primary key it\nmust be the last column of the key (so the row-key encoding stays bounded for the\npreceding columns)."},"625":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-29","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-paths","content":"All three BSON functions address fields with a small path syntax:"},"626":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-30","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-paths","content":"name — top-level field"},"627":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-31","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-paths","content":"addresses[0] — first element of an array"},"628":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-32","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-paths","content":"addresses[0].city — nested field"},"629":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-33","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-paths","content":"preferences.theme — nested field on a sub-document"},"630":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-34","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-value","content":"BSON_VALUE(bson_column, field_path, target_type [, default]) projects a single field\nout of a document and returns it as the requested Phoenix data type. Returns NULL if\nthe path is missing — or the supplied default if you provide one."},"631":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-35","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-value","content":"BSON_VALUE can also return a sub-document (use 'BSON' as the target type) and\nreturn raw binary fields as VARBINARY_ENCODED. A companion function\nBSON_VALUE_TYPE(bson_column, field_path) returns the BSON type of the field as a\nstring (e.g. 'STRING', 'INT32', 'DOCUMENT', 'ARRAY')."},"632":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-36","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"BSON_CONDITION_EXPRESSION(bson_column, expression) evaluates a boolean expression\nover a document and returns TRUE or FALSE. Use it in a WHERE clause when you\nwant to filter on multiple document fields at once, or test for the existence/shape\nof fields, without the verbosity of stacking multiple BSON_VALUE calls."},"633":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-37","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"The expression language is intentionally small and DynamoDB-flavored:"},"634":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-38","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"Comparisons: =, <> (or !=), <, <=, >, >="},"635":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-39","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"Boolean composition: AND, OR, NOT, parentheses"},"636":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-40","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"Ranges and sets: BETWEEN ... AND ..., IN (...), IS NULL"},"637":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-41","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"Field presence: field_exists(name), field_not_exists(name)"},"638":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-42","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"String/array helpers: begins_with(field, value), contains(field, value)"},"639":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-43","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"Type and size: field_type(field, 'STRING' | 'NUMBER' | ...), size(field)"},"640":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-44","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-condition-expression","content":"The expression is evaluated server-side, so rows are filtered before they cross the\nnetwork."},"641":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-45","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"BSON_UPDATE_EXPRESSION(bson_column, expression) returns a new BSON document with\nthe requested mutations applied. The intended use is inside an\natomic upsert so an entire family of per-field updates\nruns under the row lock in a single round-trip:"},"642":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-46","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"Supported update verbs follow the DynamoDB convention:"},"643":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-47","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"SET path = value — write a field (or nested path)."},"644":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-48","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"REMOVE path[, path ...] — delete a field."},"645":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-49","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"ADD path number — atomic numeric increment / decrement on a single field."},"646":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-50","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"DELETE path value — remove a value from an array/set field."},"647":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-51","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"if_not_exists(path, default) — read-then-write helper for conditional defaults."},"648":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-52","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-update-expression","content":"Because the update runs on the server under the row lock, this is the right primitive\nfor things like per-document counters, idempotent flag flips, and DynamoDB-style\nconditional writes (combine with BSON_CONDITION_EXPRESSION in a WHERE clause)."},"649":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-53","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-indexing","content":"To make a BSON-field filter fast, create a functional index on the projection:"},"650":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-54","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-indexing","content":"Combine with INCLUDE (...) to cover other projected columns, or rely on\nuncovered indexes when you only\nfilter on the indexed field."},"651":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-55","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-end-to-end","content":"Putting the pieces together — a customer-preferences scenario backed entirely by a\nsingle BSON column:"},"652":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-56","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-limitations","content":"A BSON column in a composite primary key must be the last PK column."},"653":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-57","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-limitations","content":"The condition/update expression language is intentionally a small document-focused\nDSL, not full SQL. Combine it with regular SQL predicates on relational columns when\nyou need joins, aggregations, or expressions across multiple rows."},"654":{"id":"/docs/features/bson-58","page_id":"/docs/features/bson","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bson#bson-limitations","content":"Functional indexes on BSON_VALUE(...) are scoped to one specific path/type pair —\nyou'll typically want an index per hot field rather than a generic \"BSON index\"."},"655":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"page","content":"Bulk Loading","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading"},"656":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-0","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","content":"Load Phoenix tables with PSQL and MapReduce-based CSV/JSON bulk loaders, including options, permissions, and tuning notes."},"657":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-1","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#sample-data","content":"Sample data"},"658":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-2","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#loading-via-psql","content":"Loading via PSQL"},"659":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-3","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#loading-via-mapreduce","content":"Loading via MapReduce"},"660":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-4","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#notes-on-the-mapreduce-importer","content":"Notes on the MapReduce importer"},"661":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-5","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#permission-issues-when-uploading-hfiles","content":"Permission issues when uploading HFiles"},"662":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-6","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#loading-array-data","content":"Loading array data"},"663":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-7","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#a-note-on-separator-characters","content":"A note on separator characters"},"664":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-8","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading#a-note-on-lowercase-tableschema-names","content":"A note on lowercase table/schema names"},"665":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-9","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","content":"Phoenix provides two methods for bulk loading data into Phoenix tables:"},"666":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-10","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","content":"Single-threaded client loading tool for CSV-formatted data via the psql command"},"667":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-11","page_id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/bulk-loading","content":"MapReduce-based bulk load tool for CSV and JSON-formatted data"},"668":{"id":"/docs/features/bulk-loading-12","page_id"