/features/change-data-capture-0","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","content":"Query row-level change events on a Phoenix table using standard SQL — pull pre/post images and change deltas with bounded, resumable reads."},"738":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-1","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-when","content":"When to use it"},"739":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-2","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Enabling CDC on a table"},"740":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-3","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"Reading change events"},"741":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-4","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#time-bounded--incremental-reads","content":"Time-bounded / incremental reads"},"742":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-5","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#per-partition-reads","content":"Per-partition reads"},"743":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-6","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"Stream lineage: partitions, splits, and merges"},"744":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-7","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#consuming-events-in-parent--child-order","content":"Consuming events in parent → child order"},"745":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-8","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-special-events","content":"Special event types"},"746":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-9","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-ops","content":"Operational notes"},"747":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-10","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","content":"Change Data Capture turns a Phoenix table into a stream of change events that\ndownstream consumers can read using regular SQL. Once enabled, a CDC object behaves\nlike a queryable Phoenix table: every insert, update, and delete on the underlying\ndata table produces a row containing the affected primary key, an event timestamp,\nand a JSON payload describing what changed. Available in Phoenix 5.3.0\n(PHOENIX-7001)."},"748":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-11","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-when","content":"Reach for CDC when something downstream needs to react to row-level changes:"},"749":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-12","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-when","content":"Replication or mirroring to another store (Kafka, search index, data warehouse)."},"750":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-13","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-when","content":"Audit logs and change history."},"751":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-14","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-when","content":"Cache invalidation and event-driven workflows."},"752":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-15","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-when","content":"Materialized projections / fan-out tables maintained by an external process."},"753":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-16","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-when","content":"CDC captures the actual mutations as they happen — including TTL-driven row\ndeletes — so consumers don't need to poll the data table or compute diffs."},"754":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-17","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Use CREATE CDC to enable change capture on an existing table:"},"755":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-18","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"The INCLUDE clause sets the default scopes that appear in the JSON payload.\nAvailable scopes:"},"756":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-19","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Scope"},"757":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-20","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Meaning"},"758":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-21","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"PRE"},"759":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-22","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Row image before the mutation."},"760":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-23","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"POST"},"761":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-24","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Row image after the mutation."},"762":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-25","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"CHANGE"},"763":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-26","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Just the changed columns (diff)."},"764":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-27","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"Standard CREATE INDEX–style table properties on CREATE CDC (SALT_BUCKETS,\nUPDATE_CACHE_FREQUENCY, COLUMN_ENCODED_BYTES, etc.) are forwarded to the\nunderlying CDC index."},"765":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-28","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-enable","content":"To remove CDC from a table:"},"766":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-29","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"Each CDC object behaves like a Phoenix table whose columns are:"},"767":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-30","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"The data table's primary-key columns (so you know which row changed)."},"768":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-31","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"A payload column literally named \"CDC JSON\" (case-sensitive, must be quoted).\nProject it whenever you need the change payload; omit it for lightweight queries\nthat only care about which rows changed or for topology lookups. SELECT *\nincludes it automatically."},"769":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-32","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"Two helpers come along for the ride:"},"770":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-33","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP() — the event's timestamp; use it for ordering and time-\nbounded reads."},"771":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-34","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"PARTITION_ID() — the partition the event came from; useful for sharding consumer\nworkers."},"772":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-35","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"A typical read query that wants the full payload looks like this:"},"773":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-36","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-read","content":"The CDC_INCLUDE(...) query hint overrides the default scopes set at CREATE\nCDC time — so the same CDC object can serve different downstream consumers, each\nasking for only the scope they need. Without the hint, the payload uses the\nINCLUDE scopes from the CREATE CDC statement."},"774":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-37","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#time-bounded--incremental-reads","content":"Use PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP() predicates to pull only events within a window —\nconsumers typically remember the last timestamp they processed and bind it as the\nlower bound on the next call:"},"775":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-38","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#per-partition-reads","content":"Partitions track HBase regions of the data table, so you can shard a consumer pool\nacross them. Discover partitions with:"},"776":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-39","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#per-partition-reads","content":"Then issue per-partition reads:"},"777":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-40","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"A CDC stream is logically a set of partitions, each carrying a totally-ordered\nsequence of change events. A partition isn't an abstract shard — it corresponds to a\nspecific HBase region of the data table at a point in time, and it has a finite\nlifetime: it's born when the region is created (or as the result of a split/merge)\nand it's closed when that region itself splits or merges into something new. New\nchild partitions take over from there."},"778":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-41","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"Phoenix tracks this topology in SYSTEM.CDC_STREAM. The schema makes the lineage\nexplicit — every partition row points at its parent partition(s):"},"779":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-42","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"Column"},"780":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-43","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"Notes"},"781":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-44","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"TABLE_NAME"},"782":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-45","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"The data table whose changes are streamed."},"783":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-46","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"STREAM_NAME"},"784":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-47","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"The CDC stream (each CREATE CDC produces a uniquely-named stream)."},"785":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-48","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"PARTITION_ID"},"786":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-49","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"This partition's id — same value PARTITION_ID() returns on a CDC row."},"787":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-50","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"PARENT_PARTITION_ID"},"788":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-51","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"The parent partition's id (empty/null for the initial partitions present when CDC was first enabled)."},"789":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-52","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"PARTITION_START_TIME"},"790":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-53","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"When this partition began."},"791":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-54","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"PARTITION_END_TIME"},"792":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-55","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"When it was closed (a split or merge ended it). NULL while still active."},"793":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-56","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"PARTITION_START_KEY / _END_KEY"},"794":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-57","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"The HBase region's row-key bounds at the time, stored as VARBINARY_ENCODED."},"795":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-58","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"PARENT_PARTITION_START_TIME"},"796":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-59","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"The parent partition's PARTITION_START_TIME, embedded so consumers can walk parent → child without an extra join."},"797":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-60","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"Two important shapes fall out of this model:"},"798":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-61","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"A split produces two child rows that share the same PARENT_PARTITION_ID (the\nregion that just split). Their key ranges together cover the parent's range."},"799":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-62","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-lineage","content":"A merge produces one child partition with multiple rows in SYSTEM.CDC_STREAM —\none row per parent — sharing the same PARTITION_ID and differing only in\nPARENT_PARTITION_ID. Together those rows record every parent that fed the merge."},"800":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-63","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#consuming-events-in-parent--child-order","content":"Because events from a parent partition causally precede events from any of its\nchildren, a correct consumer must drain the parent before reading children that\ndescend from it. The pattern is:"},"801":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-64","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#consuming-events-in-parent--child-order","content":"Discover the topology by reading SYSTEM.CDC_STREAM for your table and\nstream. Build a DAG keyed by PARTITION_ID, with edges drawn from\nPARENT_PARTITION_ID → child PARTITION_ID. Roots are partitions with no\nparent (the regions present when CDC was first enabled)."},"802":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-65","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#consuming-events-in-parent--child-order","content":"Process roots first, then walk forward through the DAG. A child partition is\nready to be consumed once all of its parents have been fully drained — for\nmerges, that means all rows in SYSTEM.CDC_STREAM with the same child\nPARTITION_ID have had their parent partitions processed."},"803":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-66","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#consuming-events-in-parent--child-order","content":"For each partition, query the CDC object scoped to that partition and the\ntime window the partition was live:"},"804":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-67","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#consuming-events-in-parent--child-order","content":"Re-poll the topology periodically. New rows appear in SYSTEM.CDC_STREAM\nwhen regions split or merge; consumers refresh their DAG to pick up the new\nchildren before existing partitions close."},"805":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-68","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#consuming-events-in-parent--child-order","content":"This is the same shard-lineage model used by DynamoDB Streams — if you're porting a\nDynamoDB Streams consumer, the bookkeeping translates almost directly."},"806":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-69","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-special-events","content":"TTL-driven deletes (rows aged out by time-based or\nconditional TTL) produce CDC events alongside\napplication-issued mutations, so consumers don't have to reconcile retention-driven\nremovals separately."},"807":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-70","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-special-events","content":"ARRAY and JSON columns in the data table are serialized as simple values\ninside the \"CDC JSON\" payload."},"808":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-71","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-special-events","content":"Case-sensitive identifiers on the data table and its primary-key columns are\npreserved end to end in events."},"809":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-72","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-ops","content":"When the underlying data table is dropped, its CDC stream metadata in\nSYSTEM.CDC_STREAM is cleaned up automatically — you don't have to drop the CDC\nobject first."},"810":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-73","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-ops","content":"The CDC object is internally backed by a partitioned secondary index on the data\ntable, but it's not used to satisfy regular queries against the data table —\nthere's no read penalty on the data table from enabling CDC."},"811":{"id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture-74","page_id":"/docs/features/change-data-capture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/change-data-capture#cdc-ops","content":"Re-creating CDC after a drop produces a distinct stream (the stream name is\naugmented with creation time), so consumers can detect the boundary and reset\ntheir offsets cleanly."},"812":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","type":"page","content":"Conditional TTL","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl"},"813":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-0","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","content":"Express row expiration as a SQL boolean expression evaluated against the row itself."},"814":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-1","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-when","content":"When to use it"},"815":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-2","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-define","content":"Defining a conditional TTL"},"816":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-3","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-write-path","content":"Effect on the write path"},"817":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-4","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-limitations","content":"Limitations"},"818":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-5","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","content":"Conditional TTL lets you express row expiration as a SQL boolean expression\nevaluated against the row's own column values, instead of a fixed time-since-write\nnumber. A row is considered expired the moment the expression evaluates to TRUE for\nthat row. Available in Phoenix 5.3.0\n(PHOENIX-7170)."},"819":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-6","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-when","content":"Conditional TTL fits whenever \"expired\" is an application concept, not just an\nage. Some typical cases:"},"820":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-7","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-when","content":"\"Delete this row 30 days after STATUS becomes 'CLOSED'.\""},"821":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-8","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-when","content":"\"Expire when RETAIN_UNTIL has passed.\""},"822":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-9","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-when","content":"\"Keep failed records for a week, successful ones for an hour.\""},"823":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-10","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-when","content":"\"Expire soft-deleted rows (is_deleted = TRUE) after a grace period.\""},"824":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-11","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-when","content":"If retention is a flat duration that applies uniformly to all rows, use the regular\ntime-based TTL property. If different views over the same shared table need\ndifferent retention rules, look at View TTL — it composes\nwith conditional TTL."},"825":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-12","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-define","content":"Pass a SQL boolean expression as the TTL table property at CREATE TABLE (or\nCREATE VIEW) time. The expression may reference any column of the table:"},"826":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-13","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-define","content":"Update or remove the expression later with ALTER TABLE:"},"827":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-14","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-define","content":"What \"expired\" means in practice:"},"828":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-15","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-define","content":"Read-time: any query touching the row evaluates the TTL expression server-side\nand skips the row if it returns TRUE. Rows become invisible the moment they\nmatch the predicate, regardless of physical state."},"829":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-16","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-define","content":"Phoenix compaction: expired rows are physically removed by Phoenix's\ncompaction during HBase region compactions. There is no separate cleanup job —\nphysical removal happens whenever the underlying regions compact."},"830":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-17","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-write-path","content":"IS_STRICT_TTL is a table property that applies to any kind of TTL — see\nStrict vs Relaxed TTL for the full\ndescription."},"831":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-18","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-write-path","content":"The conditional-TTL-specific cost is in the write path: with strict (the\ndefault), every mutation reads the current row state on the server to evaluate the\nTTL expression, which adds latency. With relaxed (IS_STRICT_TTL = false), that\nextra row read is skipped — at the cost of expired rows remaining visible to\nreaders until major compaction physically removes them."},"832":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-19","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-limitations","content":"Conditional TTL requires the table to have a single column family."},"833":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-20","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-limitations","content":"The TTL expression must be a valid Phoenix SQL boolean expression and may only\nreference columns of the same table — no joins, no subqueries."},"834":{"id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl-21","page_id":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/conditional-ttl#conditional-ttl-limitations","content":"The classic time-based TTL = N (seconds) and conditional TTL = '<expr>' use the\nsame property; what you pass in determines which mode you get. You can't have both\nat once."},"835":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","type":"page","content":"Cursor","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/cursor"},"836":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-0","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/cursor","content":"Use CURSOR statements in Phoenix to process query results in controlled row batches."},"837":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-1","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/cursor#using-a-cursor","content":"Using a cursor"},"838":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-2","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/cursor","content":"To work on a subset of rows from a query, Phoenix supports a CURSOR control structure. The sequence below shows how to use a cursor."},"839":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-3","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/cursor#using-a-cursor","content":"Define a cursor for a query using the DECLARE statement."},"840":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-4","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/cursor#using-a-cursor","content":"Open the cursor."},"841":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-5","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/cursor#using-a-cursor","content":"Fetch a subset of rows to work with."},"842":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-6","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/cursor#using-a-cursor","content":"Iterate through the fetched rows and process them as required."},"843":{"id":"/docs/features/cursor-7","page_id":"/docs/features/cursor","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/cursor#using-a-cursor","content":"Fetch additional sets of rows as needed, and close the cursor when done."},"844":{"id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","page_id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","type":"page","content":"Dynamic Columns","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns"},"845":{"id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns-0","page_id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","content":"Define and query late-bound dynamic columns in Phoenix when full schema cannot be declared upfront."},"846":{"id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns-1","page_id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","content":"Sometimes defining a static schema up front is not feasible. Instead, a subset of columns may be specified at table create time while the rest are specified at query time. As of Phoenix 1.2, dynamic columns are supported by allowing column definitions in parentheses after the table name in the FROM clause of a SELECT statement. Although this is not standard SQL, it is useful for leveraging the late-binding capability of HBase."},"847":{"id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns-2","page_id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","content":"For example:"},"848":{"id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns-3","page_id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","content":"You might define only a subset of event columns at create time, because each event type can have different properties:"},"849":{"id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns-4","page_id":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/dynamic-columns","content":"To upsert a row with dynamic columns:"},"850":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","type":"page","content":"Metrics","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/metrics"},"851":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-0","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics","content":"Request-level and global Phoenix client metrics, with practical usage patterns and Java examples."},"852":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-1","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/metrics#request-level-metrics","content":"Request-level metrics"},"853":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-2","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/metrics#mutation-metrics","content":"Mutation metrics"},"854":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-3","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"Scan task metrics"},"855":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-4","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/metrics#overall-query-metrics","content":"Overall query metrics"},"856":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-5","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"How to use SQL statement-level metrics"},"857":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-6","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics","content":"Phoenix surfaces various metrics that provide an insight into what is going on within the Phoenix client as it is executing various SQL statements. These metrics are collected within the client JVM in two ways:"},"858":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-7","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics","content":"Request level metrics - collected at an individual SQL statement level"},"859":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-8","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics","content":"Global metrics - collected at the client JVM level"},"860":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-9","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics","content":"Request level metrics are helpful for figuring out at a more granular level about the amount of work done by every SQL statement executed by Phoenix. These metrics can be classified into three categories:"},"861":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-10","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#mutation-metrics","content":"MUTATION_BATCH_SIZE - Batch sizes of mutations"},"862":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-11","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#mutation-metrics","content":"MUTATION_BYTES - Size of mutations in bytes"},"863":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-12","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#mutation-metrics","content":"MUTATION_COMMIT_TIME - Time it took to commit mutations"},"864":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-13","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"NUM_PARALLEL_SCANS - Number of scans executed in parallel"},"865":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-14","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"SCAN_BYTES - Number of bytes read by scans"},"866":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-15","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"MEMORY_CHUNK_BYTES - Number of bytes allocated by the memory manager"},"867":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-16","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"MEMORY_WAIT_TIME - Time in milliseconds threads needed to wait for memory to be allocated through memory manager"},"868":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-17","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"SPOOL_FILE_SIZE - Size of spool files created in bytes"},"869":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-18","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"SPOOL_FILE_COUNTER - Number of spool files created"},"870":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-19","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"CACHE_REFRESH_SPLITS_COUNTER - Number of times Phoenix's metadata cache was refreshed because of splits"},"871":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-20","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"TASK_QUEUE_WAIT_TIME - Time in milliseconds tasks had to wait in the queue of the thread pool executor"},"872":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-21","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"TASK_END_TO_END_TIME - Time in milliseconds spent by tasks from creation to completion"},"873":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-22","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"TASK_EXECUTION_TIME - Time in milliseconds tasks took to execute"},"874":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-23","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"TASK_EXECUTED_COUNTER - Counter for number of tasks submitted to the thread pool executor"},"875":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-24","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#scan-task-metrics","content":"TASK_REJECTED_COUNTER - Counter for number of tasks that were rejected by the thread pool executor"},"876":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-25","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#overall-query-metrics","content":"QUERY_TIMEOUT_COUNTER - Number of times query timed out"},"877":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-26","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#overall-query-metrics","content":"QUERY_FAILED_COUNTER - Number of times query failed"},"878":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-27","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#overall-query-metrics","content":"WALL_CLOCK_TIME_MS - Wall clock time elapsed for the overall query execution"},"879":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-28","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#overall-query-metrics","content":"RESULT_SET_TIME_MS - Wall clock time elapsed for reading all records using resultSet.next()"},"880":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-29","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Log and report query execution details which could be later used for analysis."},"881":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-30","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Report top SQL queries by duration. Metric to use: WALL_CLOCK_TIME_MS."},"882":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-31","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Check if the query is failing because it is timing out. Metric to use: QUERY_TIMEOUT_COUNTER > 0."},"883":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-32","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Monitor the amount of bytes being written to or read from HBase for a SQL statement. Metrics to use: MUTATION_BYTES and SCAN_BYTES."},"884":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-33","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Check if the query is doing too much work or needs tuning. Possible metrics to use: TASK_EXECUTED_COUNTER, TASK_QUEUE_WAIT_TIME, WALL_CLOCK_TIME_MS."},"885":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-34","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Check if a successful query is facing thread starvation, i.e., number of threads in the thread pool likely needs to be increased. This is characterized by a relatively large difference between TASK_EXECUTION_TIME and TASK_END_TO_END_TIME."},"886":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-35","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Request level metrics can be turned on/off for every Phoenix JDBC connection. Below is an example of how you can do that:"},"887":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-36","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"A typical pattern for how one could get hold of read metrics for queries:"},"888":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-37","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"One could also get hold of write related metrics (collected per table) for DML statements by doing something like this:"},"889":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-38","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Global metrics on the other hand are collected at the Phoenix client’s JVM level. These metrics could be used for building out a trend and seeing what is going on within Phoenix from client’s perspective over time. Other than the metrics reported above for request level metrics, the global metrics also includes the following counters:"},"890":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-39","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"MUTATION_SQL_COUNTER - Counter for number of mutation SQL statements"},"891":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-40","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"SELECT_SQL_COUNTER - Counter for number of SQL queries"},"892":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-41","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"OPEN_PHOENIX_CONNECTIONS_COUNTER - Number of open Phoenix connections"},"893":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-42","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Global metrics could be helpful in monitoring and tuning various aspects of the execution environment. For example: an increase in TASK_REJECTED_COUNTER is probably a symptom of too much work being submitted, or that the Phoenix thread pool queue depth or number of threads (or both) needs to be increased. Similarly, a spike in TASK_EXECUTION_TIME for a time frame could be symptomatic of several things including overloaded region servers, a network glitch, or client/region servers undergoing garbage collection."},"894":{"id":"/docs/features/metrics-43","page_id":"/docs/features/metrics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/metrics#how-to-use-sql-statement-level-metrics","content":"Collection of global client metrics can be turned on/off (on by default) by setting the attribute phoenix.query.global.metrics.enabled to true/false in the client side hbase-site.xml.\nBelow is a code snippet showing how to log/report global metrics by using a scheduled job that runs periodically:"},"895":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","type":"page","content":"Multi-tenancy","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy"},"896":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-0","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","content":"Use multi-tenant tables, tenant-specific connections, and tenant views to isolate tenant data in Phoenix."},"897":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-1","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#highlights","content":"Highlights"},"898":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-2","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#multi-tenant-tables","content":"Multi-tenant tables"},"899":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-3","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-connections","content":"Tenant-specific connections"},"900":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-4","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-views-optional","content":"Tenant-specific views (optional)"},"901":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-5","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-data-isolation","content":"Tenant data isolation"},"902":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-6","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#highlights","content":"Multi-tenancy in Phoenix works via a combination of multi-tenant tables and tenant-specific connections (detailed below)."},"903":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-7","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#highlights","content":"Tenants open tenant-specific connections to Phoenix. These connections can only access data that belongs to the tenant."},"904":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-8","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#highlights","content":"Tenants only see their own data in multi-tenant tables and can see all data in regular tables."},"905":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-9","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#highlights","content":"In order to add their own columns, tenants create tenant-specific views on top of multi-tenant tables and add their own columns to the views."},"906":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-10","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#multi-tenant-tables","content":"Multi-tenant tables in Phoenix are regular tables declared with the MULTI_TENANT=true DDL property. They work in conjunction with tenant-specific connections (detailed below) to ensure tenants only see their own data in these tables. The first primary key column of a multi-tenant table identifies the tenant. For example:"},"907":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-11","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#multi-tenant-tables","content":"The column that identifies the tenant may have any name, but it must be of type VARCHAR or CHAR. Regular Phoenix connections work with these tables without tenant constraints, including access across tenant boundaries."},"908":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-12","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-connections","content":"Tenants are identified by the presence or absence of the TenantId property at JDBC connection time. A connection with a non-null TenantId is tenant-specific. A connection with an unspecified or null TenantId is a regular connection. A tenant-specific connection may query only:"},"909":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-13","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-connections","content":"all data in non-multi-tenant (global) tables, that is, tables created with a regular connection without MULTI_TENANT=true."},"910":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-14","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-connections","content":"their own data in multi-tenant tables."},"911":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-15","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-connections","content":"their own schema, meaning it sees only tenant-specific views created by that tenant (detailed below)."},"912":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-16","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-connections","content":"For example, a tenant-specific connection is established like this:"},"913":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-17","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-views-optional","content":"Tenant-specific views may only be created using tenant-specific connections. They are created the same way as views, however the base table must be a multi-tenant table or another view that eventually points to one. Tenant-specific views are typically used when new columns and/or filter criteria, specific to that tenant, are required. Otherwise the base table may be used directly through a tenant-specific connection as described above."},"914":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-18","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-views-optional","content":"For example, a tenant-specific view may be defined as follows:"},"915":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-19","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-specific-views-optional","content":"The tenant_id column is neither visible nor accessible from a tenant-specific view. Any reference to it causes a ColumnNotFoundException. As with any Phoenix view, whether the view is updatable follows the rules described here. In addition, indexes may be added to tenant-specific views just like regular tables and views (with these limitations)."},"916":{"id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy-20","page_id":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/multi-tenancy#tenant-data-isolation","content":"Any DML or query performed on multi-tenant tables using a tenant-specific connection is automatically constrained to that tenant’s data. For UPSERT, Phoenix automatically populates the tenant_id column with the tenant ID specified at connection time. For query and DELETE operations, a WHERE clause is transparently added so operations only see data for the current tenant."},"917":{"id":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping","page_id":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping","type":"page","content":"Namespace Mapping","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping"},"918":{"id":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping-0","page_i