s-a-namespace-and-what-are-the-benefits-of-mapping-tables-to-namespaces","content":"Region server groups - A namespace/table can be pinned to a subset of RegionServers, guaranteeing a coarse level of isolation."},"990":{"id":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping-72","page_id":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping#what-is-a-namespace-and-what-are-the-benefits-of-mapping-tables-to-namespaces","content":"Details about namespace management can be read here."},"991":{"id":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping-73","page_id":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/namespace-mapping#namespace-mapping-resources","content":"PHOENIX-1311: Implementation details and discussion for the namespace mapping feature."},"992":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","type":"page","content":"Paged Queries","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/paged-queries"},"993":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-0","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries","content":"Use row value constructors and OFFSET/LIMIT for efficient paged query patterns in Phoenix."},"994":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-1","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#row-value-constructors-rvc","content":"Row Value Constructors (RVC)"},"995":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-2","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#offset-with-limit","content":"OFFSET with LIMIT"},"996":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-3","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries","content":"Phoenix supports standard SQL constructs to enable paged queries:"},"997":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-4","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries","content":"Row Value Constructors (RVC)"},"998":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-5","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries","content":"OFFSET with LIMIT"},"999":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-6","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#row-value-constructors-rvc","content":"A row value constructor is an ordered sequence of values delimited by parentheses. For example:"},"1000":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-7","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#row-value-constructors-rvc","content":"Just like regular values, row value constructors may be used in comparison expressions:"},"1001":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-8","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#row-value-constructors-rvc","content":"Row value constructors are compared by conceptually concatenating the values together and comparing them against each other, with the leftmost part being most significant. Section 8.2 (comparison predicates) of the SQL-92 standard explains this in detail, but here are a few examples of predicates that would evaluate to true:"},"1002":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-9","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#row-value-constructors-rvc","content":"Row value constructors may also be used in an IN list expression to efficiently query for a set of rows given the composite primary key columns. For example, the following would be optimized to be a point get of three rows:"},"1003":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-10","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#row-value-constructors-rvc","content":"Another primary use case for row value constructors is to support query-more type functionality by enabling an ordered set of rows to be incrementally stepped through. For example, the following query would step through a set of rows, 20 rows at a time:"},"1004":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-11","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#row-value-constructors-rvc","content":"Assuming that the client binds the three bind variables to the values of the last row processed, the next invocation would find the next 20 rows that match the query. If the columns you supply in your row value constructor match in order the columns from your primary key (or from a secondary index), then Phoenix will be able to turn the row value constructor expression into the start row of your scan. This enables a very efficient mechanism to locate at or after a row."},"1005":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-12","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#offset-with-limit","content":"Use OFFSET to specify the starting row offset into the result set returned by your query and LIMIT to specify the page size."},"1006":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-13","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#offset-with-limit","content":"For example, if page size is 10, then to select the second page, the following query can be used (rows 11 to 20 are returned):"},"1007":{"id":"/docs/features/paged-queries-14","page_id":"/docs/features/paged-queries","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/paged-queries#offset-with-limit","content":"OFFSET reads and skips rows on either the server or client depending on query type, whereas RVC is more effective for queries on the primary key axis because it can start directly from the provided key."},"1008":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","type":"page","content":"PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP()","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp"},"1009":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-0","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","content":"Read the per-row last-modified timestamp Phoenix maintains automatically — usable in projections, predicates, and indexes for fast time-bounded reads."},"1010":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-1","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-read","content":"Reading the row timestamp"},"1011":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-2","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-where","content":"In WHERE predicates"},"1012":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-3","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-index","content":"Indexing on PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP()"},"1013":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-4","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Relationship to ROW_TIMESTAMP column"},"1014":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-5","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","content":"PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP() is a built-in SQL function that returns the timestamp\nof the row's empty column, which Phoenix updates automatically on every write.\nIt's effectively the row's last-modified time, available on any Phoenix table\nwithout you having to declare or manage a timestamp column yourself. The return\ntype is DATE."},"1015":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-6","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","content":"It can be used in three places, and the third one is what makes it especially\npowerful:"},"1016":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-7","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","content":"As a projection in SELECT."},"1017":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-8","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","content":"As a predicate in WHERE (and JOIN) clauses."},"1018":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-9","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","content":"As the indexed expression in a functional index, which makes time-bounded reads fast even when the table isn't ordered by time."},"1019":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-10","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-read","content":"Project it like any other column:"},"1020":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-11","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-read","content":"The function takes no arguments and is evaluated server-side from the empty cell\nthat Phoenix already maintains for every row."},"1021":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-12","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-where","content":"Use it to bound queries by mutation time, including incremental \"what changed\nsince\" patterns:"},"1022":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-13","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-where","content":"Without an index, these predicates require a full scan of the table. The next\nsection fixes that."},"1023":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-14","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-index","content":"Create a functional index\non the function to make time-bounded reads fast. Phoenix can then seek directly\non the indexed timestamp instead of scanning the data table:"},"1024":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-15","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-index","content":"This is the typical recipe for \"scan rows changed in the last N minutes\" type\nqueries on a table whose primary key isn't time-ordered. Pair with\nuncovered indexes when you\ndon't want to duplicate payload columns into the index."},"1025":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-16","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-index","content":"If your downstream needs a continuous, ordered stream of changes rather than\nperiodic time-bounded scans — including the actual mutation deltas — reach for\nChange Data Capture instead. The functional\nindex pattern here is best suited to ad-hoc time-window reads from the same\nclient that issues regular SQL queries."},"1026":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-17","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Two related concepts share the word \"timestamp\" — they are not the same:"},"1027":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-18","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Feature"},"1028":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-19","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Direction"},"1029":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-20","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"What it does"},"1030":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-21","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"ROW_TIMESTAMP column"},"1031":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-22","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Write"},"1032":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-23","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Designate a primary-key column whose value is written into the underlying HBase row timestamp."},"1033":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-24","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP() function"},"1034":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-25","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Read"},"1035":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-26","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"Return the underlying HBase row timestamp Phoenix already maintains on every row's empty cell."},"1036":{"id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp-27","page_id":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/phoenix-row-timestamp#phoenix-row-timestamp-vs-row-timestamp","content":"PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP() works on any Phoenix table, regardless of whether\nyou've declared a ROW_TIMESTAMP column. If you have declared one, both\nmechanisms read/write the same underlying timestamp, so PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP()\nreturns the value of the ROW_TIMESTAMP column for that row."},"1037":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"page","content":"Query Server","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server"},"1038":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-0","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server","content":"Deploy and use Phoenix Query Server (PQS), including client access, impersonation, metrics, and load-balancing configuration."},"1039":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-1","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-overview","content":"Overview"},"1040":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-2","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#clients","content":"Clients"},"1041":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-3","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-installation","content":"Installation"},"1042":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-4","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-usage","content":"Usage"},"1043":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-5","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"Server"},"1044":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-6","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"Client"},"1045":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-7","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#wire-api-documentation","content":"Wire API documentation"},"1046":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-8","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#impersonation","content":"Impersonation"},"1047":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-9","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-metrics","content":"Metrics"},"1048":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-10","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-configuration","content":"Configuration"},"1049":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-11","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"Server Instantiation"},"1050":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-12","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"HTTPS"},"1051":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-13","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#secure-cluster-connection","content":"Secure Cluster Connection"},"1052":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-14","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-connection-cache","content":"Server Connection Cache"},"1053":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-15","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-statement-cache","content":"Server Statement Cache"},"1054":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-16","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#impersonation-1","content":"Impersonation"},"1055":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-17","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-metrics-config","content":"Metrics"},"1056":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-18","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-additions","content":"Query Server Additions"},"1057":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-19","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/query-server#load-balancing","content":"Load balancing"},"1058":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-20","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server","content":"The Phoenix Query Server provides an alternative means for interaction with\nPhoenix and HBase."},"1059":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-21","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-overview","content":"Phoenix 4.4 introduces a stand-alone server that exposes Phoenix to \"thin\"\nclients. It is based on the Avatica component of\nApache Calcite. The query server is comprised of a Java server that\nmanages Phoenix Connections on the clients' behalf."},"1060":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-22","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-overview","content":"With the introduction of the\nProtobuf transport, Avatica is moving towards backwards compatibility with the\nprovided thin JDBC driver. There are no such backwards compatibility guarantees\nfor the JSON API."},"1061":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-23","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-overview","content":"To repeat, there is no guarantee of backwards compatibility with the JSON transport;\nhowever, compatibility with the Protobuf transport is stabilizing (although, not\ntested thoroughly enough to be stated as \"guaranteed\")."},"1062":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-24","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#clients","content":"The primary client implementation\nis currently a JDBC driver with minimal dependencies. The default and\nprimary transport mechanism since Phoenix 4.7 is Protobuf, the older JSON mechanism can still\nbe enabled.\nThe distribution includes the sqlline-thin.py CLI client that uses the JDBC thin client."},"1063":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-25","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#clients","content":"The Phoenix project also maintains the Python driver\nphoenixdb."},"1064":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-26","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#clients","content":"The Avatica Go client\ncan also be used."},"1065":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-27","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#clients","content":"Proprietary ODBC drivers are also available for Windows and Linux."},"1066":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-28","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-installation","content":"In the 4.4-4.14 and 5.0 releases the query server and its JDBC client are part of the standard Phoenix\ndistribution. They require no additional dependencies or installation."},"1067":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-29","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-installation","content":"After the 4.15 and 5.1 release, the query server has been unbundled into the phoenix-queryserver\nrepository, and its version number has been reset to 6.0."},"1068":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-30","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-installation","content":"Download the latest source or binary release from the\nDownload page,\nor check out the development version from\nGitHub."},"1069":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-31","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-installation","content":"Either unpack the binary distribution, or build it from source. See BUILDING.md\nin the source distribution on how to build."},"1070":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-32","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"The standalone Query Server distribution does not contain the necessary\nPhoenix (thick) client library by default."},"1071":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-33","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"If using the standalone library you will either need to rebuild it from source to include the\nclient library (See BUILDING.md), or manually copy the phoenix thick client library\ninto the installation directory."},"1072":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-34","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"The server component is managed through bin/queryserver.py. Its usage is as\nfollows"},"1073":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-35","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"When invoked with no arguments, the query server is launched in the foreground,\nwith logging directed to the console."},"1074":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-36","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"The first argument is an optional start or stop command to the daemon. When\neither of these are provided, it will take appropriate action on a daemon\nprocess, if it exists."},"1075":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-37","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"Any subsequent arguments are passed to the main class for interpretation."},"1076":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-38","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server","content":"The server is packaged in a standalone jar,\nphoenix-queryserver-<version>.jar. This jar, the phoenix-client.jar and HBASE_CONF_DIR on the\nclasspath are all that is required to launch the server."},"1077":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-39","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"Phoenix provides two mechanisms for interacting with the query server. A JDBC\ndriver is provided in the standalone\nphoenix-queryserver-client-<version>.jar. The script\nbin/sqlline-thin.py is available for the command line."},"1078":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-40","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"The JDBC connection string is composed as follows:"},"1079":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-41","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"<scheme> specifies the transport protocol (http or https) used when communicating with the\nserver."},"1080":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-42","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"<server-hostname> is the name of the host offering the service."},"1081":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-43","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"<port> is the port number on which the host is listening. Default is 8765,\nthough this is configurable (see below)."},"1082":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-44","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"The full list of options that can be provided via the JDBC URL string is available\nin the Avatica documentation."},"1083":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-45","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"The script bin/sqlline-thin.py is intended to behave identically to its\nsibling script bin/sqlline.py. It supports the following usage options."},"1084":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-46","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"The first optional argument is a connection URL, as described previously. When\nnot provided, scheme defaults to http, host to localhost, and port to\n8765."},"1085":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-47","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#client","content":"The second optional parameter is a sql file from which to read commands."},"1086":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-48","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#wire-api-documentation","content":"The API itself is documented in the Apache Calcite project as it is the Avatica\nAPI -- there is no wire API defined in Phoenix itself."},"1087":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-49","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#wire-api-documentation","content":"JSON API"},"1088":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-50","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#wire-api-documentation","content":"Protocol Buffer API"},"1089":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-51","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#wire-api-documentation","content":"For more information in building clients in other languages that work with\nAvatica, please feel free to reach out to the Apache Calcite dev mailing list."},"1090":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-52","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#impersonation","content":"By default, the Phoenix Query Server executes queries on behalf of the end-user. HBase permissions\nare enforced given the end-user, not the Phoenix Query Server's identity. In some cases, it may\nbe desirable to execute the query as some other user -- this is referred to as \"impersonation\".\nThis can enable workflows where a trusted user has the privilege to run queries for other users."},"1091":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-53","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#impersonation","content":"This can be enabled by setting the configuration property phoenix.queryserver.withRemoteUserExtractor\nto true. The URL of the Query Server can be modified to include the required request parameter.\nFor example, to let \"bob\" to run a query as \"alice\", the following JDBC URL could be used:"},"1092":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-54","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#impersonation","content":"The standard Hadoop \"proxyuser\" configuration keys are checked to validate if the \"real\" remote user\nis allowed to impersonate the \"doAs\" user. See the Hadoop documentation\nfor more information on how to configure these rules."},"1093":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-55","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#impersonation","content":"As a word of warning: there is no end-to-end test coverage for the HBase 0.98 and 1.1 Phoenix releases\nbecause of missing test-related code in those HBase releases. While we expect no issues on these\nPhoenix release lines, we recommend additional testing by the user to verify that there are no issues."},"1094":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-56","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-metrics","content":"By default, the Phoenix Query Server exposes various Phoenix global client metrics via JMX (for HBase versions 1.3 and up).\nThe list of metrics are available here."},"1095":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-57","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-metrics","content":"PQS Metrics use Hadoop Metrics 2 internally for metrics publishing. Hence it publishes various JVM related metrics. Metrics can be filtered based on certain tags, which can be configured by the property specified in hbase-site.xml on the classpath. Further details are provided in Configuration section."},"1096":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-58","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-configuration","content":"Server components are spread across a number of java packages, so effective\nlogging configuration requires updating multiple packages. The default server\nlogging configuration sets the following log levels:"},"1097":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-59","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#query-server-configuration","content":"As of the time of writing, the underlying Avatica component respects the\nfollowing configuration options exposed via hbase-site.xml."},"1098":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-60","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"Property"},"1099":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-61","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"Description"},"1100":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-62","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"Default"},"1101":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-63","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"phoenix.queryserver.http.port"},"1102":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-64","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"Port the server listens on."},"1103":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-65","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"8765"},"1104":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-66","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"phoenix.queryserver.metafactory.class"},"1105":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-67","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"Avatica Meta.Factory implementation class."},"1106":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-68","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"org.apache.phoenix.queryserver.server.PhoenixMetaFactoryImpl"},"1107":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-69","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"phoenix.queryserver.serialization"},"1108":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-70","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"Transport/serialization format (PROTOBUF or JSON)."},"1109":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-71","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#server-instantiation","content":"PROTOBUF"},"1110":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-72","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"HTTPS support is only available in unbundled phoenix-queryserver versions."},"1111":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-73","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Property"},"1112":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-74","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Description"},"1113":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-75","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Default"},"1114":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-76","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"phoenix.queryserver.tls.enabled"},"1115":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-77","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Enables HTTPS transport. When enabled, keystore/truststore files and passwords are also required."},"1116":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-78","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"false"},"1117":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-79","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"phoenix.queryserver.tls.keystore"},"1118":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-80","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Keystore file containing the HTTPS private key."},"1119":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-81","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"unset"},"1120":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-82","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"phoenix.queryserver.tls.keystore.password"},"1121":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-83","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Password for HTTPS keystore."},"1122":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-84","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"empty string"},"1123":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-85","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"phoenix.queryserver.tls.truststore"},"1124":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-86","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Keystore file containing the HTTPS certificate."},"1125":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-87","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"unset"},"1126":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-88","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"phoenix.queryserver.tls.truststore.password"},"1127":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-89","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"Password for HTTPS truststore."},"1128":{"id":"/docs/features/query-server-90","page_id":"/docs/features/query-server","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#https","content":"empty string"},"1