"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/query-server#load-balancing","content":"phoenix"},"1232":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","type":"page","content":"Row Timestamp Column","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column"},"1233":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-0","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"Map HBase row timestamps to a Phoenix primary-key column and use ROW_TIMESTAMP in table design and upserts."},"1234":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-1","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#sample-schema","content":"Sample schema"},"1235":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-2","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#see-also","content":"See also"},"1236":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-3","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"Phoenix 4.6 provides a way to map HBase’s native row timestamp to a Phoenix column. This helps you take advantage of HBase optimizations for time ranges in store files, along with query optimizations built into Phoenix."},"1237":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-4","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"For a column to be designated as ROW_TIMESTAMP, certain constraints need to be followed:"},"1238":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-5","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"Only a primary key column of type TIME, DATE, TIMESTAMP, BIGINT, or UNSIGNED_LONG can be designated as ROW_TIMESTAMP."},"1239":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-6","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"Only one primary key column can be designated as ROW_TIMESTAMP."},"1240":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-7","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"The column value cannot be NULL (because it maps directly to the HBase row timestamp). This also means a column can be declared as ROW_TIMESTAMP only when creating the table."},"1241":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-8","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"A ROW_TIMESTAMP column value cannot be negative. For DATE/TIME/TIMESTAMP, the corresponding epoch time in milliseconds cannot be less than zero."},"1242":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-9","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","content":"When upserting rows for a table with a row timestamp column (using UPSERT VALUES or UPSERT SELECT), you can explicitly provide the row timestamp value or let Phoenix set it automatically. When not specified, Phoenix sets the row timestamp column value to server-side time. The value also becomes the timestamp of the corresponding row in HBase."},"1243":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-10","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#sample-schema","content":"This sets CREATED_DATE to the value specified in the corresponding bind parameter."},"1244":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-11","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#sample-schema","content":"This sets CREATED_DATE to server-side time."},"1245":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-12","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#sample-schema","content":"This sets CREATED_DATE to the DATE selected from SOURCE_METRICS_TABLE."},"1246":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-13","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#sample-schema","content":"This sets CREATED_DATE in the destination table to the server timestamp."},"1247":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-14","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#sample-schema","content":"When querying with filters on the row timestamp column, Phoenix performs its usual row-key optimizations and can also set scan min/max time ranges appropriately. Using this time range information, HBase can skip store files that do not fall in the target time range, which significantly improves performance, especially for tail-end data queries."},"1248":{"id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column-15","page_id":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/row-timestamp-column#see-also","content":"The PHOENIX_ROW_TIMESTAMP() function lets you read the underlying HBase row timestamp on any Phoenix table — even one without a declared ROW_TIMESTAMP column — and is indexable for fast time-bounded queries."},"1249":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","type":"page","content":"Salted Tables","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/salted-tables"},"1250":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-0","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables","content":"Use SALT_BUCKETS to reduce HBase hotspotting and understand scan, split, and ordering behavior for salted tables."},"1251":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-1","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#sequential-scan","content":"Sequential scan"},"1252":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-2","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#splitting","content":"Splitting"},"1253":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-3","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#row-key-ordering","content":"Row key ordering"},"1254":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-4","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#salted-tables-performance","content":"Performance"},"1255":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-5","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables","content":"Sequential writes in HBase may suffer from region server hotspotting if your row key is monotonically increasing. Salting the row key helps mitigate this problem. See this article for details."},"1256":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-6","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables","content":"Phoenix provides a way to transparently salt the row key with a salting byte for a table. Specify this at table creation time with the SALT_BUCKETS table property, using a value from 1 to 256:"},"1257":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-7","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables","content":"There are some behavior differences and cautions to be aware of when using a salted table."},"1258":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-8","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#sequential-scan","content":"Since a salted table does not store data in natural key sequence, a strict sequential scan does not return data in natural sorted order. Clauses that force sequential scan behavior (for example, LIMIT) may return rows differently compared to a non-salted table."},"1259":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-9","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#splitting","content":"If no split points are specified, a salted table is pre-split on salt-byte boundaries to ensure load distribution across region servers, including during initial table growth. If split points are provided manually, they must include the salt byte."},"1260":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-10","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#row-key-ordering","content":"Pre-splitting also ensures that entries in each region start with the same salt byte and are therefore locally sorted. During a parallel scan across regions, Phoenix can use this property to perform a client-side merge sort. The resulting scan can still be returned sequentially, as if from a normal table."},"1261":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-11","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#row-key-ordering","content":"This row-key ordered scan can be enabled by setting phoenix.query.rowKeyOrderSaltedTable=true in hbase-site.xml. When enabled, user-specified split points on salted tables are disallowed to ensure each bucket contains only entries with the same salt byte. With this property enabled, a salted table behaves more like a normal table for scans and returns items in row-key order."},"1262":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-12","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#salted-tables-performance","content":"Using salted tables with pre-splitting helps distribute write workload uniformly across region servers, which improves write performance. Our performance evaluation shows that salted tables can achieve up to 80% higher write throughput than non-salted tables."},"1263":{"id":"/docs/features/salted-tables-13","page_id":"/docs/features/salted-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/salted-tables#salted-tables-performance","content":"Reads from salted tables can also benefit from more uniform data distribution. Our performance evaluation shows improved read performance for queries focused on subsets of data."},"1264":{"id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes","page_id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes","type":"page","content":"Secondary Indexes","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes"},"1265":{"id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes-0","page_id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes","content":"Concepts, setup, consistency guarantees, and tooling for Phoenix secondary indexes."},"1266":{"id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes-1","page_id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes#covered-indexes","content":"Covered Indexes"},"1267":{"id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes-2","page_id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes#functional-indexes","content":"Functional Indexes"},"1268":{"id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes-3","page_id":"/docs/features/secondary-indexes","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/d