on."},"2027":{"id":"/docs/functions-112","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#first_values","content":"Example"},"2028":{"id":"/docs/functions-113","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#last_values","content":"Returns an array of at most the given numeric size of the last values in each distinct group ordered according to the ORDER BY specification."},"2029":{"id":"/docs/functions-114","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#last_values","content":"Example"},"2030":{"id":"/docs/functions-115","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#nth_value","content":"The nth value in each distinct group ordered according to the ORDER BY specification."},"2031":{"id":"/docs/functions-116","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#nth_value","content":"Example"},"2032":{"id":"/docs/functions-117","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#stddev_pop","content":"The population standard deviation of all values. Aggregates are only allowed in select statements. The returned value is of decimal data type."},"2033":{"id":"/docs/functions-118","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#stddev_pop","content":"Example"},"2034":{"id":"/docs/functions-119","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#stddev_samp","content":"The sample standard deviation of all values. Aggregates are only allowed in select statements. The returned value is of decimal data type."},"2035":{"id":"/docs/functions-120","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#stddev_samp","content":"Example"},"2036":{"id":"/docs/functions-121","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#round","content":"Rounds the numeric or timestamp expression to the nearest scale or time unit specified. If the expression is a numeric type, then the second argument is the scale to be used for rounding off the number, defaulting to zero. If the expression is a date/time type, then the second argument may be one of the time units listed to determine the remaining precision of the date/time. A default of MILLISECONDS is used if not present. The multiplier is only applicable for a date/time type and is used to round to a multiple of a time unit (i.e. 10 minute) and defaults to 1 if not specified. This method returns the same type as its first argument."},"2037":{"id":"/docs/functions-122","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#round","content":"Example"},"2038":{"id":"/docs/functions-123","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#ceil","content":"Same as ROUND, except it rounds any fractional value up to the next even multiple."},"2039":{"id":"/docs/functions-124","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#ceil","content":"Example"},"2040":{"id":"/docs/functions-125","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#floor","content":"Same as ROUND, except it rounds any fractional value down to the previous even multiple."},"2041":{"id":"/docs/functions-126","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#floor","content":"Example"},"2042":{"id":"/docs/functions-127","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#trunc","content":"Same as FLOOR"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/functions-128","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#trunc","content":"Example"},"2044":{"id":"/docs/functions-129","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_number","content":"Formats a string or date/time type as a number, optionally accepting a format string. For details on the format, see java.text.DecimalFormat. For date, time, and timeStamp terms, the result is the time in milliseconds since the epoch. This method returns a decimal number."},"2045":{"id":"/docs/functions-130","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_number","content":"Example"},"2046":{"id":"/docs/functions-131","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#rand","content":"Function that produces a random, uniformly distributed double value between 0.0 (inclusive) and 1.0 (exclusive). If a seed is provided, then the the returned value is identical across each invocation for the same row. If a seed is not provided, then the returned value is different for each invocation. The seed must be a constant."},"2047":{"id":"/docs/functions-132","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#rand","content":"Example"},"2048":{"id":"/docs/functions-133","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#substr","content":"Returns a substring of a string starting at the one-based position. If zero is used, the position is zero-based. If the start index is negative, then the start index is relative to the end of the string. The length is optional and if not supplied, the rest of the string will be returned."},"2049":{"id":"/docs/functions-134","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#substr","content":"Example"},"2050":{"id":"/docs/functions-135","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#instr","content":"Returns the one-based position of the initial occurrence of the second argument in the first argument. If the second argument is not contained in the first argument, then zero is returned."},"2051":{"id":"/docs/functions-136","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#instr","content":"Example"},"2052":{"id":"/docs/functions-137","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#trim","content":"Removes leading and trailing spaces from the input string."},"2053":{"id":"/docs/functions-138","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#trim","content":"Example"},"2054":{"id":"/docs/functions-139","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#ltrim","content":"Removes leading spaces from the input string."},"2055":{"id":"/docs/functions-140","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#ltrim","content":"Example"},"2056":{"id":"/docs/functions-141","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#rtrim","content":"Removes trailing spaces from the input string."},"2057":{"id":"/docs/functions-142","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#rtrim","content":"Example"},"2058":{"id":"/docs/functions-143","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#lpad","content":"Pads the string expression with the specific pad character (space by default) up to the length argument."},"2059":{"id":"/docs/functions-144","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#lpad","content":"Example"},"2060":{"id":"/docs/functions-145","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#length","content":"Returns the length of the string in characters."},"2061":{"id":"/docs/functions-146","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#length","content":"Example"},"2062":{"id":"/docs/functions-147","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#regexp_substr","content":"Returns a substring of a string by applying a regular expression start from the offset of a one-based position. Just like with SUBSTR, if the start index is negative, then it is relative to the end of the string. If not specified, the start index defaults to 1."},"2063":{"id":"/docs/functions-148","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#regexp_substr","content":"Example"},"2064":{"id":"/docs/functions-149","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#regexp_replace","content":"Returns a string by applying a regular expression and replacing the matches with the replacement string. If the replacement string is not specified, it defaults to an empty string."},"2065":{"id":"/docs/functions-150","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#regexp_replace","content":"Example"},"2066":{"id":"/docs/functions-151","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#regexp_split","content":"Splits a string into a VARCHAR ARRAY using a regular expression. If characters that have a special meaning in regular expressions are to be used as a regular delimiter in the pattern string, they must be escaped with backslashes."},"2067":{"id":"/docs/functions-152","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#regexp_split","content":"Example"},"2068":{"id":"/docs/functions-153","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#upper","content":"Returns upper case string of the string argument. If localeString (available in Phoenix 4.14) is provided, it identifies the locale whose rules are used for the conversion. If localeString is not provided, the default locale is used. The localeString must be of the form returned by the Java 6 implementation of java.util.Locale.toString() e.g. 'zh_TW_STROKE' or 'en_US' or 'fr_FR'."},"2069":{"id":"/docs/functions-154","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#upper","content":"Example"},"2070":{"id":"/docs/functions-155","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#lower","content":"Returns lower case string of the string argument. If localeString (available in Phoenix 4.14) is provided, it identifies the locale whose rules are used for the conversion. If localeString is not provided, the default locale is used. The localeString must be of the form returned by the Java 6 implementation of java.util.Locale.toString() e.g. 'zh_TW_STROKE' or 'en_US' or 'fr_FR'."},"2071":{"id":"/docs/functions-156","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#lower","content":"Example"},"2072":{"id":"/docs/functions-157","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#reverse","content":"Returns reversed string of the string argument."},"2073":{"id":"/docs/functions-158","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#reverse","content":"Example"},"2074":{"id":"/docs/functions-159","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_char","content":"Formats a date, time, timestamp, or number as a string. The default date format is \"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\" and the default number format is \"#,##0.###\". For details, see java.text.SimpleDateFormat for date/time values and java.text.DecimalFormat for numbers. This method returns a string."},"2075":{"id":"/docs/functions-160","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_char","content":"Example"},"2076":{"id":"/docs/functions-161","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#collation_key","content":"Calculates a collation key that can be used to sort strings in a natural-language-aware way. The localeString must be of the form returned by the Java 6 implementation of java.util.Locale.toString() e.g. 'zh_TW_STROKE' or 'en_US' or 'fr_FR'. The third, fourth and fifth arguments are optional and determine respectively whether to use a special upper-case collator, the strength value of the collator, and the decomposition value of the collator. (See java.text.Collator to learn about strength and decomposition)."},"2077":{"id":"/docs/functions-162","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#collation_key","content":"Example"},"2078":{"id":"/docs/functions-163","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_elem","content":"Alternative to using array subscript notation to access an array element. Returns the element in the array at the given position. The position is one-based."},"2079":{"id":"/docs/functions-164","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_elem","content":"Example"},"2080":{"id":"/docs/functions-165","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_length","content":"Returns the current length of the array."},"2081":{"id":"/docs/functions-166","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_length","content":"Example"},"2082":{"id":"/docs/functions-167","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_append","content":"Appends the given element to the end of the array."},"2083":{"id":"/docs/functions-168","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_append","content":"Example"},"2084":{"id":"/docs/functions-169","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_prepend","content":"Appends the given element to the beginning of the array."},"2085":{"id":"/docs/functions-170","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_prepend","content":"Example"},"2086":{"id":"/docs/functions-171","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_cat","content":"Concatenates the input arrays and returns the result."},"2087":{"id":"/docs/functions-172","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_cat","content":"Example"},"2088":{"id":"/docs/functions-173","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_fill","content":"Returns an array initialized with supplied value and length."},"2089":{"id":"/docs/functions-174","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_fill","content":"Example"},"2090":{"id":"/docs/functions-175","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_to_string","content":"Concatenates array elements using supplied delimiter and optional null string and returns the resulting string. If the nullString parameter is omitted or NULL, any null elements in the array are simply skipped and not represented in the output string."},"2091":{"id":"/docs/functions-176","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#array_to_string","content":"Example"},"2092":{"id":"/docs/functions-177","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#any","content":"Used on the right-hand side of a comparison expression to test that any array element satisfies the comparison expression against the left-hand side."},"2093":{"id":"/docs/functions-178","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#any","content":"Example"},"2094":{"id":"/docs/functions-179","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#all","content":"Used on the right-hand side of a comparison expression to test that all array elements satisfy the comparison expression against the left-hand side. of the array."},"2095":{"id":"/docs/functions-180","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#all","content":"Example"},"2096":{"id":"/docs/functions-181","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#md5","content":"Computes the MD5 hash of the argument, returning the result as a BINARY(16)."},"2097":{"id":"/docs/functions-182","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#md5","content":"Example"},"2098":{"id":"/docs/functions-183","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#invert","content":"Inverts the bits of the argument. The return type will be the same as the argument."},"2099":{"id":"/docs/functions-184","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#invert","content":"Example"},"2100":{"id":"/docs/functions-185","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#encode","content":"Encodes the expression according to the encoding format provided and returns the resulting string. For 'BASE62', converts the given base 10 number to a base 62 number and returns a string representing the number."},"2101":{"id":"/docs/functions-186","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#encode","content":"Example"},"2102":{"id":"/docs/functions-187","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#decode","content":"Decodes the expression according to the encoding format provided and returns the resulting value as a VARBINARY. For 'HEX', converts the hex string expression to its binary representation, providing a mechanism for inputting binary data through the console."},"2103":{"id":"/docs/functions-188","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#decode","content":"Example"},"2104":{"id":"/docs/functions-189","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#coalesce","content":"Returns the value of the first argument if not null and the second argument otherwise. Useful to guarantee that a column in an UPSERT SELECT command will evaluate to a non null value."},"2105":{"id":"/docs/functions-190","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#coalesce","content":"Example"},"2106":{"id":"/docs/functions-191","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#get_bit","content":"Retrieves the bit at the given index in the given binary value."},"2107":{"id":"/docs/functions-192","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#get_bit","content":"Example"},"2108":{"id":"/docs/functions-193","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#get_byte","content":"Retrieves the byte at the given index in the given binary value."},"2109":{"id":"/docs/functions-194","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#get_byte","content":"Example"},"2110":{"id":"/docs/functions-195","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#octet_length","content":"Returns the number of bytes in a binary value."},"2111":{"id":"/docs/functions-196","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#octet_length","content":"Example"},"2112":{"id":"/docs/functions-197","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#set_bit","content":"Replaces the bit at the given index in the binary value with the provided newValue."},"2113":{"id":"/docs/functions-198","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#set_bit","content":"Example"},"2114":{"id":"/docs/functions-199","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#set_byte","content":"Replaces the byte at the given index in the binary value with the provided newValue."},"2115":{"id":"/docs/functions-200","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#set_byte","content":"Example"},"2116":{"id":"/docs/functions-201","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_date","content":"Parses a string and returns a date. Note that the returned date is internally represented as the number of milliseconds since the java epoch. The most important format characters are: y year, M month, d day, H hour, m minute, s second. The default format string is \"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\". For details of the format, see java.text.SimpleDateFormat. By default, GMT will be used as the time zone when parsing the date. However, a time zone id can also be supplied. This is a time zone id such as 'GMT+1'. If 'local' is provided as the time zone id, the local time zone will be used for parsing. The configuration setting \"phoenix.query.dateFormatTimeZone\" can also be set to a time zone id, which will cause the default of GMT to be overridden with the configured time zone id. Please see the Data Type reference guide about how Apache Phoenix presently defines the DATE datatype. Additionally, Phoenix supports the ANSI SQL \"date\" literal which acts similarly to the single-argument \"TO_DATE\" function."},"2117":{"id":"/docs/functions-202","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_date","content":"Example"},"2118":{"id":"/docs/functions-203","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#current_date","content":"Returns the current server-side date, bound at the start of the execution of a query based on the current time on the region server owning the metadata of the table being queried. Please see the Data Type reference guide about how Apache Phoenix presently defines the DATE datatype."},"2119":{"id":"/docs/functions-204","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#current_date","content":"Example"},"2120":{"id":"/docs/functions-205","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_time","content":"Converts the given string into a TIME instance. When a date format is not provided it defaults to \"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS\" or whatever is defined by the configuration property phoenix.query.dateFormat. The configuration setting phoenix.query.dateFormatTimeZone can also be set to a time zone id, which will cause the default of GMT to be overridden with the configured time zone id. Additionally, Phoenix supports the ANSI SQL \"time\" literal which acts similarly to the single-argument \"TO_TIME\" function."},"2121":{"id":"/docs/functions-206","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_time","content":"Example"},"2122":{"id":"/docs/functions-207","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_timestamp","content":"Converts the given string into a TIMESTAMP instance. When a date format is not provided it defaults to \"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS\" or whatever is defined by the configuration property phoenix.query.dateFormat. The configuration setting phoenix.query.dateFormatTimeZone can also be set to a time zone id, which will cause the default of GMT to be overridden with the configured time zone id. Additionally, Phoenix supports the ANSI SQL \"timestamp\" literal which acts similarly to the single-argument \"TO_TIMESTAMP\" function."},"2123":{"id":"/docs/functions-208","page_id":"/docs/functions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/functions#to_timestamp","content":"Example"},"2124":{"id":"/docs/functions-209","page_