es from Hadoop MapReduce jobs."},"3262":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-1","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#a-stock","content":"a) stock"},"3263":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-2","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#b-stock_stats","content":"b) stock_stats"},"3264":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-3","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#below-is-a-simple-job-configuration","content":"Below is a simple job configuration"},"3265":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-4","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#job-configuration","content":"Job Configuration"},"3266":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-5","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#stockwritable","content":"StockWritable"},"3267":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-6","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#stock-mapper","content":"Stock Mapper"},"3268":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-7","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#stock-reducer","content":"Stock Reducer"},"3269":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-8","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#packaging-and-running","content":"Packaging and Running"},"3270":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-9","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"Phoenix provides support for retrieving and writing to Phoenix tables from within MapReduce jobs. The framework now provides custom InputFormat and OutputFormat classes PhoenixInputFormat , PhoenixOutputFormat."},"3271":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-10","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"PhoenixMapReduceUtil provides several utility methods to set the input and output configuration parameters to the job."},"3272":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-11","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"When a Phoenix table is the source for the MapReduce job, we can provide a SELECT query or pass a table name and specific columns to import data. To retrieve data from the table within the mapper class, we need to have a class that implements DBWritable and pass it as an argument to PhoenixMapReduceUtil.setInput. The custom DBWritable class provides an implementation for readFields(ResultSet rs) that allows us to retrieve columns for each row. This custom DBWritable class will form the input value to the mapper class."},"3273":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-12","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"Note: The SELECT query must not perform any aggregation or use DISTINCT as these are not supported by our map-reduce integration."},"3274":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-13","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"Similarly, when writing to a Phoenix table, we use PhoenixMapReduceUtil.setOutput to set the output table and columns."},"3275":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-14","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"Note: Phoenix internally builds the UPSERT query for you ."},"3276":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-15","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"The output key and value class for the job should always be NullWritable and the custom DBWritable class that implements the write method."},"3277":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-16","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"Let's dive into an example where we have a table, STOCK, that holds the master data of quarterly recordings in a double array for each year, and we want to find the max price of each stock across all years. Let's store the output in STOCK_STATS, which is another Phoenix table."},"3278":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-17","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","content":"Note: You can definitely configure a job to read from HDFS and load into a Phoenix table."},"3279":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-18","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#b-stock_stats","content":"Sample Data"},"3280":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-19","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#packaging-and-running","content":"Ensure phoenix-[version]-client.jar is in the classpath of your MapReduce job JAR."},"3281":{"id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration-20","page_id":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/map-reduce-integration#packaging-and-running","content":"To run the job, use the hadoop jar command with the necessary arguments."},"3282":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","type":"page","content":"Phoenix-DynamoDB REST Service","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Integrations"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb"},"3283":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-0","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","content":"Run a DynamoDB-compatible REST endpoint on top of Apache Phoenix using the phoenix-dynamodb module from apache/phoenix-adapters."},"3284":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-1","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-when","content":"When to use it"},"3285":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-2","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-where","content":"Where to find it"},"3286":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-3","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","content":"Phoenix-DynamoDB is a REST service that lets applications written against the\nAmazon DynamoDB HTTP API talk to a Phoenix cluster instead. It is distributed as the\nphoenix-dynamodb module of\napache/phoenix-adapters."},"3287":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-4","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","content":"This page describes when to choose it and points at the canonical source. Build\ninstructions, supported API surface, configuration reference, and deployment\nnotes live in the phoenix-adapters repo's own README and module documentation."},"3288":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-5","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-when","content":"Reach for Phoenix-DynamoDB when:"},"3289":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-6","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-when","content":"You have an existing application built against the AWS DynamoDB SDK and want to\npoint it at a Phoenix cluster without rewriting the data-access layer."},"3290":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-7","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-when","content":"You want to serve DynamoDB-style document workloads from Phoenix and pair the\nREST front-end with the BSON document type for the\nunderlying storage and indexing."},"3291":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-8","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-when","content":"You need a REST endpoint in front of a Phoenix cluster that speaks a wire\nprotocol most clients already know how to talk to."},"3292":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-9","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-when","content":"For native Phoenix workloads, prefer the standard JDBC driver — it exposes the full\nSQL surface, not just whatever subset the DynamoDB API maps onto."},"3293":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-10","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-where","content":"Source and documentation:\ngithub.com/apache/phoenix-adapters"},"3294":{"id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb-11","page_id":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/phoenix-dynamodb#phoenix-dynamodb-where","content":"Module: phoenix-dynamodb"},"3295":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","type":"page","content":"Pig Integration","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Integrations"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration"},"3296":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-0","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","content":"Use Apache Pig StoreFunc and Loader with Phoenix-backed tables."},"3297":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-1","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-storefunc","content":"Pig StoreFunc"},"3298":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-2","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"3299":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-3","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"Pig Loader"},"3300":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-4","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-integration-example","content":"Example"},"3301":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-5","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#future-work","content":"Future Work"},"3302":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-6","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","content":"Pig integration may be divided into two parts: a StoreFunc as a means to generate Phoenix-encoded data through Pig, and a Loader which enables Phoenix-encoded data to be read by Pig."},"3303":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-7","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-storefunc","content":"The StoreFunc allows users to write data in Phoenix-encoded format to HBase tables using Pig scripts. This is a nice way to bulk upload data from a MapReduce job in parallel to a Phoenix table in HBase. All you need to specify is the endpoint address, HBase table name and a batch size. For example:"},"3304":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-8","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-storefunc","content":"The above reads a file testdata and writes the elements to table CORE.ENTITY_HISTORY in HBase running on localhost. The first StoreFunc argument is the server, and the second is the batch size for Phoenix upserts. The batch size is related to how many rows you can hold in memory. A good default is 1000 rows, but if your rows are wide, you may want to decrease this."},"3305":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-9","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-storefunc","content":"Note that Pig types must be in sync with the target Phoenix data types. This StoreFunc tries best to cast based on input Pig types and target Phoenix data types, but it is recommended to provide an appropriate schema."},"3306":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-10","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#gotchas","content":"It is advised that the upsert operation be idempotent. That is, trying to re-upsert data should not cause any inconsistencies. This is important in the case when a Pig job fails in process of writing to a Phoenix table. There is no notion of rollback (due to lack of transactions in HBase), and re-trying the upsert with PhoenixHBaseStorage must result in the same data in HBase table."},"3307":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-11","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#gotchas","content":"For example, let’s assume we are writing records n1...n10 to HBase. If the job fails in the middle of this process, we are left in an inconsistent state where n1...n7 made it to the phoenix tables but n8...n10 were missed. If we retry the same operation, n1...n7 would be re-upserted and n8...n10 would be upserted this time."},"3308":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-12","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"A Pig data loader allows users to read data from Phoenix-backed HBase tables within a Pig script."},"3309":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-13","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"The LoadFunc provides two alternative ways to load data."},"3310":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-14","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"Given a table name, the following will load the data for all the columns in the HIRES table:"},"3311":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-15","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"To restrict the list of columns, you may specify the column names as part of LOAD as shown below:"},"3312":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-16","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"Here, only data for ID and NAME columns are returned."},"3313":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-17","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"Given a query, the following loads data for all those rows whose AGE column has a value of greater than 50:"},"3314":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-18","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"The LOAD func merely executes the given SQL query and returns the results. Though there is a provision to provide a query as part of LOAD, it is restricted to the following:"},"3315":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-19","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"Only a SELECT query is allowed. No DML statements such as UPSERT or DELETE."},"3316":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-20","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"The query may not contain any GROUP BY, ORDER BY, LIMIT, or DISTINCT clauses."},"3317":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-21","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"The query may not contain any AGGREGATE functions."},"3318":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-22","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"In both cases, the ZooKeeper quorum should be passed to PhoenixHBaseLoader as a constructor argument."},"3319":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-23","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-loader","content":"The LoadFunc makes a best effort to map Phoenix data types to Pig datatypes. You can check org.apache.phoenix.pig.util.TypeUtil to see how each Phoenix data type maps to Pig."},"3320":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-24","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-integration-example","content":"Determine the number of users by a CLIENT ID."},"3321":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-25","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-integration-example","content":"DDL"},"3322":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-26","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#pig-integration-example","content":"Pig Script"},"3323":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-27","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#future-work","content":"Support for ARRAY data type."},"3324":{"id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration-28","page_id":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/pig-integration#future-work","content":"Usage of expressions within the SELECT clause when providing a full query."},"3325":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","type":"page","content":"Python Driver","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Development","Integrations"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver"},"3326":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-0","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","content":"Use the Phoenix DB-API Python driver with Phoenix Query Server."},"3327":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-1","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#python-driver-installation","content":"Installation"},"3328":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-2","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#from-pypi","content":"From PyPI"},"3329":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-3","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#from-source","content":"From source"},"3330":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-4","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#python-driver-examples","content":"Examples"},"3331":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-5","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#python-driver-limitations","content":"Limitations"},"3332":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-6","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#python-driver-resources","content":"Resources"},"3333":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-7","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","content":"The Python driver for Apache Phoenix implements the Python DB 2.0 API to access Phoenix via Phoenix Query Server.\nThe driver is tested with Python 2.7 and 3.5-3.8. This code was originally called python-phoenixdb\nand was graciously donated by its authors to the Apache Phoenix project."},"3334":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-8","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","content":"All future development of the project is being done in Apache Phoenix."},"3335":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-9","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#from-pypi","content":"The latest release is always available from PyPI, and can be installed by\npip/pip3 as usual."},"3336":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-10","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#from-source","content":"You can build phoenixdb from the official source release,\nor use the latest development version from the source\nrepository. The python-phoenixdb source\nlives in the python-phoenixdb directory of the phoenix-queryserver\nrepository."},"3337":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-11","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#python-driver-limitations","content":"None presently known."},"3338":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-12","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/python-driver#python-driver-resources","content":"PHOENIX-4636: Initial landing of the driver into Apache Phoenix."},"3339":{"id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver-13","page_id":"/docs/integrations/python-driver","tags":["mult