eation which determines whether or not null values should be explicitly stored in HBase. This is a client side parameter. Available starting from Phoenix 4.3."},"4890":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1216","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"false"},"4891":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1217","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"phoenix.table.istransactional.default"},"4892":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1218","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"The default value of the TRANSACTIONAL flag used for table creation which determines whether or not a table is transactional . This is a client side parameter. Available starting from Phoenix 4.7."},"4893":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1219","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"false"},"4894":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1220","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"phoenix.transactions.enabled"},"4895":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1221","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"Determines whether or not transactions are enabled in Phoenix. A table may not be declared as transactional if transactions are disabled. This is a client side parameter. Available starting from Phoenix 4.7."},"4896":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1222","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"false"},"4897":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1223","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"phoenix.mapreduce.split.by.stats"},"4898":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1224","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"Determines whether to use the splits determined by statistics for MapReduce input splits. Default is true. This is a server side parameter. Available starting from Phoenix 4.10. Set to false to enable behavior from previous versions."},"4899":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1225","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"true"},"4900":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1226","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"phoenix.log.level"},"4901":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1227","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"Client-side property enabling query (only SELECT statement) logging. The logs are written to the SYSTEM.LOG table (requires a user to have W access on SYSTEM.LOG table). Possible values: OFF = No logging; INFO = Enables query logging; DEBUG = More details on Query (Explain plan, HBase Scan Details etc); TRACE = Logs query bind parameters as well. Available starting from Phoenix 4.14. WARNING: Enabling this feature may leak sensitive information to anyone who can access the SYSTEM.LOG table."},"4902":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1228","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"OFF"},"4903":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1229","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"phoenix.log.sample.rate"},"4904":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1230","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"Client-side property controlling the probability of logging a query to the query log. Set to a value between 0.0(no query) and 1.0(100% queries) . Available starting from Phoenix 4.14."},"4905":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1231","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-configuration","content":"1.0"},"4906":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1232","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backward-compatibility","content":"Phoenix maintains backward compatibility across at least two minor releases to allow for no downtime through server-side rolling\nrestarts upon upgrading. See below for details."},"4907":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1233","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versioning-convention","content":"Phoenix uses a standard three number versioning schema of the form:"},"4908":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1234","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versioning-convention","content":"For example, 4.2.1 has a major version of 4,\na minor version of 2, and a patch version of 1."},"4909":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1235","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#patch-release","content":"Upgrading to a new patch release (i.e. only the patch version has changed) is the simplest case. The jar upgrade may occur in any order: client first or server first, and a mix of clients with different patch release versions is fine."},"4910":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1236","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-release","content":"When upgrading to a new minor release (i.e. the major version is the same, but the minor\nversion has changed), sometimes modifications to the system tables are necessary to either\nfix a bug or provide a new feature. This upgrade will occur automatically the first time a\nnewly upgraded client connects to the newly upgraded server. It is required that the\nserver-side jar be upgraded first across your entire cluster, before any clients are\nupgraded. An older client (two minor versions back) will work with a newer server jar when\nthe minor version is different, but not vice versa. In other words, clients do not need to\nbe upgraded in lock step with the server. However, as the server version moves forward,\nthe client version should move forward as well. This allows Phoenix to evolve its client/server\nprotocol while still providing clients sufficient time to upgrade their clients."},"4911":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1237","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-release","content":"As of the 4.3 release, a mix of clients on different minor release versions is supported as well\n(note that prior releases required all clients to be upgraded at the same time). Another improvement\nas of the 4.3 release is that an upgrade may be done directly from one minor version to another\nhigher minor version (prior releases required an upgrade to each minor version in between)."},"4912":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1238","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#major-release","content":"Upgrading to a new major release may require downtime as well as potentially the running of a migration\nscript. Additionally, all clients and servers may need to be upgraded at the same time. This will be\ndetermined on a release-by-release basis."},"4913":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1239","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#release-notes","content":"Specific details on issues and their fixes that may impact you can be found here."},"4914":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1240","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance","content":"type: warning"},"4915":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1241","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance","content":"This page has not been updated recently and may not reflect the current state\nof the project."},"4916":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1242","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance","content":"Phoenix follows the philosophy of bringing the computation to the data by using:"},"4917":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1243","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance","content":"coprocessors to perform operations on the server-side thus minimizing client/server data transfer"},"4918":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1244","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance","content":"custom filters to prune data as close to the source as possible."},"4919":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1245","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance","content":"In addition, to minimize startup costs, Phoenix uses native HBase APIs rather than going through the MapReduce framework."},"4920":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1246","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-vs-related-products","content":"Below are charts showing relative performance between Phoenix and some other related products."},"4921":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1247","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-vs-hive-running-over-hdfs-and-hbase","content":"Query: SELECT COUNT(1) from a table over 10M and 100M rows. Data has 5 narrow columns. Number of region\nservers: 4 (HBase heap: 10GB, processor: 6 cores @ 3.3GHz Xeon)."},"4922":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1248","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-vs-impala-running-over-hbase","content":"Query: SELECT COUNT(1) from a table over 1M and 5M rows. Data has 3 narrow columns. Number of region servers: 1 (virtual machine, HBase heap: 2GB, processor: 2 cores @ 3.3GHz Xeon)."},"4923":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1249","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#latest-automated-performance-run","content":"Latest Automated Performance Run |\nAutomated Performance Runs History"},"4924":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1250","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#essential-column-family","content":"The Phoenix 1.2 query filter leverages the HBase Filter Essential Column Family feature. This improves performance when Phoenix filters data split across multiple column families (CFs) by loading only essential CFs first. In a second pass, all CFs are loaded as needed."},"4925":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1251","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#essential-column-family","content":"Consider the following schema in which data is split into two CFs:\nCREATE TABLE t (k VARCHAR NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, a.c1 INTEGER, b.c2 VARCHAR, b.c3 VARCHAR, b.c4 VARCHAR)."},"4926":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1252","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#essential-column-family","content":"Running a query similar to the following shows significant performance gains when a subset of rows matches the filter:\nSELECT COUNT(c2) FROM t WHERE c1 = ?"},"4927":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1253","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#essential-column-family","content":"The following chart shows in-memory query performance for the query above with 10M rows on 4 region servers, when 10% of rows match the filter. Note: cf-a is approximately 8 bytes and cf-b is approximately 400 bytes wide."},"4928":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1254","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-skip-scan","content":"Skip Scan Filter leverages HBase filter SEEK_NEXT_USING_HINT (docs). It significantly improves point queries over key columns."},"4929":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1255","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-skip-scan","content":"Consider the following schema in which data is split into two CFs:\nCREATE TABLE t (k VARCHAR NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, a.c1 INTEGER, b.c2 VARCHAR, b.c3 VARCHAR)."},"4930":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1256","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-skip-scan","content":"Running a query similar to the following shows significant performance gains when a subset of rows matches the filter:\nSELECT COUNT(c1) FROM t WHERE k IN (1% random k's)"},"4931":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1257","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-skip-scan","content":"The following chart shows in-memory query performance of the query above with 10M rows on 4 region servers when 1% random keys over the full key range are passed in the IN clause. Note: all VARCHAR columns are approximately 15 bytes."},"4932":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1258","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-salting","content":"Salting in Phoenix 1.2 improves both read and write performance by adding an extra hash byte at the start of the key and pre-splitting data into regions. This reduces hotspotting on one or a few region servers. Read more about this feature here."},"4933":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1259","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-salting","content":"Consider the following schema:"},"4934":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1260","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-salting","content":"CREATE TABLE T (HOST CHAR(2) NOT NULL,DOMAIN VARCHAR NOT NULL,\nFEATURE VARCHAR NOT NULL,DATE DATE NOT NULL,USAGE.CORE BIGINT,USAGE.DB BIGINT,STATS.ACTIVE_VISITOR\nINTEGER CONSTRAINT PK PRIMARY KEY (HOST, DOMAIN, FEATURE, DATE)) SALT_BUCKETS = 4."},"4935":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1261","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-salting","content":"The following chart shows write performance with and without salting, where the table is split into 4 regions on a 4-region-server cluster (note: for optimal performance, salt bucket count should match region server count)."},"4936":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1262","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-salting","content":"The following chart shows in-memory query performance for a 10M-row table where host='NA' matches 3.3M rows."},"4937":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1263","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-salting","content":"select count(1) from t where host='NA'"},"4938":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1264","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#top-n","content":"The following chart shows in-memory query time for a Top-N query over 10M rows using Phoenix 1.2 and Hive over HBase."},"4939":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1265","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#top-n","content":"select core from t order by core desc limit 10"},"4940":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1266","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-testing-overview","content":"Pherf is a standalone tool for performance and functional testing through Phoenix. Pherf can be used both to generate highly customized datasets and to measure SQL performance against that data."},"4941":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1267","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#running","content":"Edit config/env.sh to include the required property values."},"4942":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1268","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#running","content":"bin/pherf-standalone.py -h"},"4943":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1269","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#running","content":"Example: bin/pherf-standalone.py -drop all -l -q -z [zookeeper] -schemaFile .*user_defined_schema.sql -scenarioFile .*user_defined_scenario.xml"},"4944":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1270","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-h Help"},"4945":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1271","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-l Apply schema and load data"},"4946":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1272","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-q Executes multi-threaded query sets and writes results"},"4947":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1273","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-z [quorum] ZooKeeper quorum"},"4948":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1274","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-m Enable monitor for statistics"},"4949":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1275","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-monitorFrequency [frequency in ms] Frequency at which the monitor will snapshot stats to log file"},"4950":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1276","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-drop [pattern] Regex drop all tables with schema name as PHERF. Example drop Event tables: -drop .*(EVENT).*. Drop all: -drop .* or -drop all"},"4951":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1277","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-scenarioFile Regex or file name of a specific scenario file to run"},"4952":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1278","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-schemaFile Regex or file name of a specific schema file to run"},"4953":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1279","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-export Exports query results to CSV files in CSV_EXPORT directory"},"4954":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1280","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-diff Compares results with previously exported results"},"4955":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1281","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-hint Executes all queries with specified hint. Example: SMALL"},"4956":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1282","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-rowCountOverride"},"4957":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1283","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pherf-arguments","content":"-rowCountOverride [number of rows] Specify number of rows to be upserted rather than using row count specified in schema"},"4958":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1284","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Review test_scenario.xml\nfor syntax examples."},"4959":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1285","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Rules are defined as <columns /> and are applied in the order they appear in file."},"4960":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1286","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Rules of the same type override the values of a prior rule of the same type. If <userDefined>true</userDefined> is\nset, rule will only\napply override when type and name match the column name in Phoenix."},"4961":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1287","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"<prefix> tag is set at the column level. It can be used to define a constant string appended to the beginning of\nCHAR and VARCHAR data type values."},"4962":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1288","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Required field Supported Phoenix types: VARCHAR, CHAR, DATE, DECIMAL, INTEGER"},"4963":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1289","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"denoted by the <type> tag"},"4964":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1290","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"User defined true changes rule matching to use both name and type fields to determine equivalence."},"4965":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1291","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Default is false if not specified and equivalence will be determined by type only. An important note here is that you can still override rules without the user defined flag, but they will change the rule globally and not just for a specified column."},"4966":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1292","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Required field Supported Data Sequences"},"4967":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1293","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"RANDOM: Random value which can be bound by other fields such as length."},"4968":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1294","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"SEQUENTIAL: Monotonically increasing long prepended to random strings."},"4969":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1295","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Only supported on VARCHAR and CHAR types"},"4970":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1296","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"LIST: Means pick values from predefined list of values"},"4971":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1297","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Required field Length defines boundary for random values for CHAR and VARCHAR types."},"4972":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1298","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"denoted by the <length> tag"},"4973":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1299","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Column level Min/Max value defines boundaries for numerical values. For DATEs, these values supply a range between\nwhich values are generated. At the column level the granularity is a year. At a specific data value level, the\ngranularity is down to the Ms."},"4974":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1300","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"denoted by the <minValue> tag"},"4975":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1301","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"denoted by the <maxValue> tag"},"4976":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1302","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Null chance denotes the probability of generating a null value. From [0-100]. The higher the number, the more likely\nthe value will be null. Denoted by <nullChance>."},"4977":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1303","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Name can either be any text or the actual column name in the Phoenix table."},"4978":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1304","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"denoted by the <name> tag"},"4979":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1305","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Value List is used in conjunction with LIST data sequences. Each entry is a DataValue with a specified value to be\nused when generating data."},"4980":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1306","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"Denoted by the <valueList><datavalue><value/></datavalue></valueList> tags"},"4981":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1307","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"If the distribution attribute on the datavalue is set, values will be created according to\nthat probability."},"4982":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1308","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"When distribution is used, values must add up to 100%."},"4983":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1309","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adding-rules-for-data-creation","content":"If distribution is not used, values will be randomly picked from the list with equal distribution."},"4984":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1310","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-scenario","content":"A scenario can have multiple querySets. Consider the following example: concurrency of 1-4 means that each query will be\nexecuted starting with concurrency level of 1 and reach up to maximum concurrency of 4. Per thread, query would be\nexecuted to a minimum of 10 times or 10 seconds (whichever comes first). QuerySet by default is executed serially but you\ncan change executionType to PARALLEL so queries are executed concurrently. Each Query may have an optional timeoutDuration\nfield that defines the amount of time (in milliseconds) before execution for that Query is cancelled. Scenarios are defined\nin XML files stored in the resource directory."},"4985":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1311","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#results","content":"Results are written real time in results directory. Open the result that is saved in .jpg format for real time\nvisualization. Results are written using DataModelResult objects, which are modified over the course of each Pherf\nrun."},"4986":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1312","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#xml-results","content":"Pherf XML results have a similar format to the corresponding scenario.xml file used for the Pherf run, but also include\nadditional information, such as the execution time of queries, whether queries timed out, and result row count."},"4987":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1313","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#csv-results","content":"Each row in a CSV result file represents a single execution of a query and provides details about a query execution's\nruntime, timeout status, result row count, and more. The header file format can be found in Header.java."},"4988":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1314","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing","content":"Default quorum is localhost. If you want to override set the system variable."},"4989":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1315","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing","content":"Run unit tests: mvn test -DZK_QUORUM=localhostRun a specific method: mvn -Dtest=ClassName#methodName test"},"4990":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1316","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing","content":"More to come..."},"4991":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1317","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration","content":"The phoenix-spark plugin extends Phoenix's MapReduce support to allow Spark to load Phoenix tables\nas DataFrames, and enables persisting them back to Phoenix."},"4992":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1318","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-prerequisites","content":"Phoenix 4.4.0+"},"4993":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1319","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-prerequisites","content":"Spark 1.3.1+ (prebuilt with Hadoop 2.4 recommended)"},"4994":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1320","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-not-jdbc","content":"Although Spark supports connecting directly to JDBC databases, it's only able to parallelize\nqueries by partitioning on a numeric column. It also requires a known lower bound, upper bound\nand partition count in order to create split queries."},"4995":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1321","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-not-jdbc","content":"In contrast, the phoenix-spark integration is able to leverage the underlying splits provided by\nPhoenix in order to retrieve and save data across multiple workers. All that's required is a\ndatabase URL and a table name. Optional SELECT columns can be given, as well as pushdown predicates\nfor efficient filtering."},"4996":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1322","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-not-jdbc","content":"The choice of which method to use to access Phoenix comes down to each specific use case."},"4997":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1323","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-setup","content":"To ensure that all requisite Phoenix/HBase platform dependencies are available on the classpath\nfor Spark executors and drivers, set both spark.executor.extraClassPath and\nspark.driver.extraClassPath in spark-defaults.conf to include\nphoenix-<version>-client.jar."},"4998":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1324","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-setup","content":"Note that for Phoenix versions 4.7 and 4.8 you must use the 'phoenix-<version>-client-spark.jar'."},"4999":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1325","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-setup","content":"As of Phoenix 4.10, phoenix-<version>-client.jar is compiled against Spark 2.x.\nIf compatibility with Spark 1.x is needed, compile Phoenix with the spark16 Maven profile."},"5000":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1326","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-setup","content":"To help your IDE, you can add the following provided dependency to your build:"},"5001":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1327","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-setup","content":"As of Phoenix 4.15.0, the connectors project is separated from the main Phoenix project (see phoenix-connectors)\nand will have its own releases. You can add the following dependency in your project:"},"5002":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1328","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-setup","content":"The first released connectors jar is connectors-1.0.0 (replace phoenix.connectors.version above with this version)."},"5003":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1329","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reading-phoenix-tables","content":"Given a Phoenix table with the following DDL and DML:"},"5004":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1330","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#load-as-a-dataframe-using-the-datasourcev2-api","content":"Scala example:"},"5005":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1331","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#load-as-a-dataframe-using-the-datasourcev2-api","content":"Java example:"},"5006":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1332","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-dataframes-to-phoenix-using-datasourcev2","content":"The save method on DataFrame allows passing in a data source type. You can use\nphoenix for DataSourceV2 and must also pass in a table and zkUrl parameter to\nspecify which table and server to persist the DataFrame to. The column names are derived from\nthe DataFrame's schema field names, and must match the Phoenix column names."},"5007":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1333","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-dataframes-to-phoenix-using-datasourcev2","content":"The save method also takes a SaveMode option, for which only SaveMode.Overwrite is supported."},"5008":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1334","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-dataframes-to-phoenix-using-datasourcev2","content":"Given two Phoenix tables with the following DDL:"},"5009":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1335","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-dataframes-to-phoenix-using-datasourcev2","content":"you can load from an input table and save to an output table as a DataFrame as follows in Scala:"},"5010":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1336","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-dataframes-to-phoenix-using-datasourcev2","content":"Java example:"},"5011":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1337","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-from-an-external-rdd-with-a-schema-to-a-phoenix-table","content":"Just like the previous example, you can pass in the data source type as phoenix and specify the table and\nzkUrl parameters indicating which table and server to persist the DataFrame to."},"5012":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1338","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-from-an-external-rdd-with-a-schema-to-a-phoenix-table","content":"Note that the schema of the RDD must match its column data and this must match the schema of the Phoenix table\nthat you save to."},"5013":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1339","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-from-an-external-rdd-with-a-schema-to-a-phoenix-table","content":"Given an output Phoenix table with the following DDL:"},"5014":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1340","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-from-an-external-rdd-with-a-schema-to-a-phoenix-table","content":"you can save a dataframe from an RDD as follows in Scala:"},"5015":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1341","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-from-an-external-rdd-with-a-schema-to-a-phoenix-table","content":"Java example:"},"5016":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1342","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pyspark","content":"With Spark's DataFrame support, you can also use pyspark to read and write from Phoenix tables."},"5017":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1343","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#load-a-dataframe","content":"Given a table TABLE1 and a ZooKeeper URL of phoenix-server:2181, you can load the table as a\nDataFrame using the following Python code in pyspark:"},"5018":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1344","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-a-dataframe","content":"Given the same table and ZooKeeper URL above, you can save a DataFrame to a Phoenix table\nusing the following code:"},"5019":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1345","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-notes","content":"If you want to use DataSourceV1, you can use source type \"org.apache.phoenix.spark\"\ninstead of \"phoenix\", however this is deprecated as of connectors-1.0.0."},"5020":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1346","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-notes","content":"The (deprecated) functions phoenixTableAsDataFrame, phoenixTableAsRDD and saveToPhoenix all support\noptionally specifying a conf Hadoop configuration parameter with custom Phoenix client settings,\nas well as an optional zkUrl parameter for the Phoenix connection URL."},"5021":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1347","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-notes","content":"If zkUrl isn't specified, it's assumed that the \"hbase.zookeeper.quorum\" property has been set\nin the conf parameter. Similarly, if no configuration is passed in, zkUrl must be specified."},"5022":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1348","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-notes","content":"As of PHOENIX-5197, you can pass configurations from the driver\nto executors as a comma-separated list against the key phoenixConfigs i.e (PhoenixDataSource.PHOENIX_CONFIGS), for ex:"},"5023":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1349","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-notes","content":"This list of properties is parsed and populated into a properties map which is passed to DriverManager.getConnection(connString, propsMap).\nNote that the same property values will be used for both the driver and all executors and\nthese configurations are used each time a connection is made (both on the driver and executors)."},"5024":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1350","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-limitations","content":"Basic support for column and predicate pushdown using the Data Source API"},"5025":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1351","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-limitations","content":"The Data Source API does not support passing custom Phoenix settings in configuration, you must\ncreate the DataFrame or RDD directly if you need fine-grained configuration."},"5026":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1352","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-integration-limitations","content":"No support for aggregate or distinct queries as explained in our Map Reduce Integration documentation."},"5027":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1353","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pagerank-example","content":"This example makes use of the Enron email data set, provided by the\nStanford Network Analysis Project,\nand executes the GraphX implementation of PageRank on it to find interesting entities. It then\nsaves the results back to Phoenix."},"5028":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1354","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pagerank-example","content":"Download and extract the file enron.csv.gz"},"5029":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1355","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pagerank-example","content":"Create the necessary Phoenix schema"},"5030":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1356","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pagerank-example","content":"Load the email data into Phoenix (assuming localhost for ZooKeeper quorum URL)"},"5031":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1357","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pagerank-example","content":"In spark-shell, with the phoenix-client in the Spark driver classpath, run the following:"},"5032":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1358","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pagerank-example","content":"Query the top ranked entities in SQL"},"5033":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1359","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#saving-rdds-to-phoenix","content":"saveToPhoenix is an implicit method on RDD[Product], or an RDD of Tuples. The data types must\ncorrespond to the Java types Phoenix supports Datatypes"},"5034":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1360","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#saving-rdds-to-phoenix","content":"Given a Phoenix table with the following DDL:"},"5035":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1361","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive","content":"The Apache Phoenix Storage Handler is a plugin that enables Apache Hive access to Phoenix tables from the Apache Hive command line using HiveQL."},"5036":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1362","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-prerequisites","content":"This document describes the plugin available in the phoenix-connectors source repo, as of December 2023."},"5037":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1363","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-prerequisites","content":"Phoenix 5.1.0+"},"5038":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1364","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-prerequisites","content":"Hive 3.1.2+"},"5039":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1365","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-prerequisites","content":"phoenix-connectors 6.0.0-SNAPSHOT"},"5040":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1366","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-prerequisites","content":"The Phoenix Storage handler currently only supports Hive 3.1.\nIt has only been tested with Hive Hive 3.1.3, and Phoenix 5.1.3."},"5041":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1367","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-prerequisites","content":"A variant for Hive 4 is planned after Hive 4.0.0 is released."},"5042":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1368","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"The Phoenix Storage Handler used to be part of the main Phoenix repo, but it has been refactored into the separate phoenix-connectors repo after the release of Phoenix 5.0.\nAt the time of writing there is no released version of the connectors project, so it must be built from the Git source repository HEAD."},"5043":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1369","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"Official releases will be available on the Downloads page."},"5044":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1370","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"Check the value of the hbase.version property in the root pom.xml.\nIf it is older than HBase 2.5.0, then you need to rebuild HBase locally as described\nin BUILDING.md in the main Phoenix repository."},"5045":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1371","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"Check out the HEAD version of https://github.com/apache/phoenix-connectors.\nBuild it with:"},"5046":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1372","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"The binary distribution will be created in phoenix5-connectors-assembly/target."},"5047":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1373","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"The driver is built for HBase 2.4.x by default."},"5048":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1374","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"To build it for other HBase versions, set hbase.version, hbase.compat.version, hadoop.version, zookeeper.version, and hbase-thirdparty-version to the versions used by HBase. hbase.compat.version is the matching hbase-compat module in Phoenix; other versions can be copied from the root pom.xml in the HBase source."},"5049":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1375","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#apache-hive-building","content":"For example, to build with HBase 2.1.10 (rebuilt with -Dhadoop.profile=3.0), use:"},"5050":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1376","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"Hive 3.1 ships with HBase 2.0 beta, which is incompatible with Phoenix.\nTo use the Phoenix Storage handler, HBase and its dependencies have to be removed from Hive."},"5051":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1377","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"To remove the shipped HBase 2.0 beta perform the following on each Hive node:"},"5052":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1378","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"Create a backup of the Hive /lib directory"},"5053":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1379","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"Remove the HBase dependencies from the /lib directory:"},"5054":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1380","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"Even though Hive ships with HBase jars, it includes a mechanism to load the hbase mapredcp jars automatically."},"5055":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1381","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"To ensure that Hive finds and uses the correct HBase JARs and hbase-site.xml, set the following\nenvironment variables in hive-env.sh on each node, or make sure they are set properly in the environment:"},"5056":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1382","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"HBASE_HOME: The root directory of the HBase installation."},"5057":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1383","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"HBASE_CONF_DIR: The directory where hbase-site.xml resides. Defaults to /etc/hbase/conf, or if it does not exist, to $HBASE_HOME/conf."},"5058":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1384","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"HBASE_BIN_DIR: The directory that holds the hbase command. Defaults to $HBASE_HOME/bin."},"5059":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1385","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-hive-3","content":"It is assumed that the Hadoop variables are already set correctly, and the HBase libraries and up-to-date hbase-site.xml are available on each Hive node."},"5060":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1386","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hive-setup","content":"It is necessary to make the phoenix5-hive-shaded-6.0.0-SNAPSHOT-shaded.jar available for every hive component.\nThere are many ways to achieve this; one of the simpler options is to use the HIVE_AUX_JARS_PATH environment variable."},"5061":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1387","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hive-setup","content":"If hive-env.sh already sets HIVE_AUX_JARS_PATH, then copy the connector JAR there.\nOtherwise, create a world-readable directory on the system and copy the connector JAR there. Then add:"},"5062":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1388","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hive-setup","content":"to hive-env.sh."},"5063":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1389","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hive-setup","content":"This must be performed on every Hive node."},"5064":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1390","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-creation-and-deletion","content":"The Phoenix Storage Handler supports only EXTERNAL Hive tables."},"5065":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1391","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#create-external-table","content":"For EXTERNAL tables, Hive works with an existing Phoenix table and manages only Hive metadata. Dropping an EXTERNAL table from Hive deletes only Hive metadata but does not delete the Phoenix table."},"5066":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1392","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#properties","content":"phoenix.table.name"},"5067":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1393","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#properties","content":"Specifies the Phoenix table name"},"5068":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1394","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#properties","content":"Default: the same as the Hive table"},"5069":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1395","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#properties","content":"phoenix.zookeeper.quorum"},"5070":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1396","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#properties","content":"Specifies the Zo