y should be auto-generated for the primary key column."},"5248":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1574","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#kafka-plugin-configuration","content":"Note: This plugin supports Phoenix Flume event serializers."},"5249":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1575","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#kafka-plugin-configuration","content":"RegexEventSerializer parses Kafka messages based on the regex specified in the configuration file."},"5250":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1576","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#kafka-plugin-configuration","content":"JsonEventSerializer parses Kafka messages based on the schema specified in the configuration file."},"5251":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1577","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#python-driver","content":"The Python driver for Apache Phoenix implements the Python DB 2.0 API to access Phoenix via Phoenix Query Server.\nThe driver is tested with Python 2.7 and 3.5-3.8. This code was originally called python-phoenixdb\nand was graciously donated by its authors to the Apache Phoenix project."},"5252":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1578","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#python-driver","content":"All future development of the project is being done in Apache Phoenix."},"5253":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1579","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#from-pypi","content":"The latest release is always available from PyPI, and can be installed by\npip/pip3 as usual."},"5254":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1580","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#from-source","content":"You can build phoenixdb from the official source release,\nor use the latest development version from the source\nrepository. The python-phoenixdb source\nlives in the python-phoenixdb directory of the phoenix-queryserver\nrepository."},"5255":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1581","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#python-driver-limitations","content":"None presently known."},"5256":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1582","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#python-driver-resources","content":"PHOENIX-4636: Initial landing of the driver into Apache Phoenix."},"5257":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1583","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#python-driver-resources","content":"PHOENIX-4688: Implementation of Kerberos authentication via SPNEGO."},"5258":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1584","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-orm-library","content":"PHO is an Object Relational Mapping (ORM) library for building and executing queries on HBase using Apache Phoenix.\nIt provides ORM-style mappings and DSL-style query building. Initially developed and open sourced by eHarmony, it is available on GitHub."},"5259":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1585","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-orm-library","content":"Its interfaces and generic annotations make it possible to switch data store APIs in the future without changing query definitions.\nCurrently, it supports HBase integration through Apache Phoenix, and can be extended with other implementations."},"5260":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1586","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#entity-class","content":"Suppose we have the following TestClass we want to query in our data store:"},"5261":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1587","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-building","content":"Query building can be done in DSL style. More advanced query building is under development, but for now we will use a combination of QueryBuilder and static, Hibernate-style Restrictions methods to construct queries."},"5262":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1588","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#simple-queries","content":"Construct a query to find all user matches delivered in the past two days and not in a closed state."},"5263":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1589","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compound-queries","content":"Construct a more complex query where we find items older than one day, include multiple status values, order by deliveryDate, and limit result size to 10."},"5264":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1590","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compound-queries","content":"By default, expressions are combined with AND when added separately."},"5265":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1591","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-interface","content":"The following query components are supported:"},"5266":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1592","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#resolving-entity-and-property-names","content":"Always use the property names of your Java objects in your queries.\nIf these names differ from those used in your datastore, use annotations to provide mappings.\nEntity resolvers are configured to map entity classes to table or collection names.\nProperty resolvers are configured to map object variable names to column or field names."},"5267":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1593","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#resolving-entity-and-property-names","content":"The following annotations are currently supported for the indicated data store type.\nCustom EntityResolvers and PropertyResolvers are straightforward to configure and create."},"5268":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1594","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#resolving-entity-and-property-names","content":"See Morphia annotations for entity class annotation mappings."},"5269":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1595","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-execution","content":"The QueryExecutor interface supports the following operations:"},"5270":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1596","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-orm-library-configuration","content":"Here are some example Spring configuration files for HBase using Apache Phoenix."},"5271":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1597","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase","content":"Configuration properties:"},"5272":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1598","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-omid-transaction-manager","content":"The Apache Omid podling has recently decided to graduate as a sub-project of Apache Phoenix."},"5273":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1599","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-omid-transaction-manager","content":"This graduation process is currently ongoing. Please follow the Apache Phoenix mailing lists\nfor more information about this effort."},"5274":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1600","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-tephra-transaction-manager","content":"The Apache Tephra podling has recently decided to graduate as a sub-project of Apache Phoenix."},"5275":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1601","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#phoenix-tephra-transaction-manager","content":"This graduation process is currently ongoing. Please follow the Apache Phoenix mailing lists\nfor more information about this effort."},"5276":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1602","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Transactions (beta). Above and beyond the row-level transactional semantics of HBase, Phoenix adds cross row and cross table transaction support with full ACID semantics by integrating with Tephra, now an Apache incubator project. Tephra provides snapshot isolation of concurrent transactions by implementing multi-versioned concurrency control."},"5277":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1603","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Set up a system to use transactions in Phoenix with the following steps:"},"5278":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1604","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Add the following config to your client-side hbase-site.xml file to enable transactions:"},"5279":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1605","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Add the following config to your server-side hbase-site.xml file to configure the transaction manager.\nThe \"Transaction Server Configuration\" section of Tephra describes the available configuration options."},"5280":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1606","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Also set the transaction timeout (time after which open transactions become invalid) to a reasonable value:"},"5281":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1607","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Set $HBASE_HOME and start the transaction manager:"},"5282":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1608","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"The transaction manager would typically be configured to run on one or more of the master nodes in your HBase cluster."},"5283":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1609","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Once this setup is done, transactions may then be enabled on a table by table basis by using the TRANSACTIONAL=true property when you create your table:"},"5284":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1610","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"An existing table may also be altered to be transactional, but be careful because you cannot switch a transactional table back to being non transactional:"},"5285":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1611","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"A transaction is started implicitly through the execution of a statement on a transactional table and then finished through either a commit or rollback. Once started, the statements will not see any data committed by other transactions until the transaction is complete. They will, however, see their own uncommitted data. For example:"},"5286":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1612","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"An exception is thrown if a transaction tries to commit a row that conflicts with other overlapping transaction that already committed. For example:"},"5287":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1613","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"In a second transaction perform a commit for the same row."},"5288":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1614","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Now if you try to commit the first transaction you will get an exception"},"5289":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1615","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Queries are only able to view commits that completed before the current transaction started and are not able to view the in progress changes of other transactions."},"5290":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1616","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"Indexes added to a transactional table are transactional as well with regard to their incremental maintenance. For example, the following index added to my_table will be kept transactional consistent with its data table as mutations are made:"},"5291":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1617","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"During a commit, if either the index or data table write fails, an exception is thrown and the client can either roll back or retry.\nIf the commit fails both the index and data table rows are not visible."},"5292":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1618","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions","content":"An external Tephra transaction that has already been started can be used with Phoenix by setting the transaction context of the Phoenix connection:"},"5293":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1619","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions-limitations","content":"Starting a transaction on a connection with an SCN set is not allowed."},"5294":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1620","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions-limitations","content":"Setting the maximum number of versions property while creating a transactional table limits the number of snapshots available for concurrent transactions."},"5295":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1621","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions-limitations","content":"When a transaction times out or if it cannot be rolled back by the client, it is added to an invalid list. This list can potentially grow if there are a lot of failed or timed out transactions.\nFor now, an administrator can manually clear transactions from this list after a major compaction has occurred. TEPHRA-35 describes ongoing work to automatically remove transactions from the invalid list once all data associated with the transaction has been removed."},"5296":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1622","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transactions-limitations","content":"If adding an index asynchronously to an existing transactional table, make sure to run a major compaction before issuing the CREATE INDEX ASYNC command as otherwise invalid and/or uncommitted transactions may appear in your index PHOENIX-2154."},"5297":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1623","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions","content":"As of Phoenix 4.4.0 we have added the ability to allow users to create and deploy\ntheir own custom or domain-specific UDFs to the cluster."},"5298":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1624","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-overview","content":"Users can create temporary or permanent user-defined (domain-specific) scalar functions.\nUDFs can be used like built-in functions in queries such as SELECT, UPSERT, DELETE, and when creating functional indexes.\nTemporary functions are session-scoped and are not accessible from other sessions.\nPermanent function metadata is stored in the SYSTEM.FUNCTION table.\nPhoenix also supports tenant-specific functions. Functions created in one tenant-specific connection are not visible to other tenant-specific connections.\nOnly global tenant (no-tenant) functions are visible to all connections."},"5299":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1625","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-overview","content":"Phoenix leverages the HBase dynamic class loader to load UDF JARs from HDFS at the Phoenix client and region server without restarting services."},"5300":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1626","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-configuration","content":"Add the following parameters to hbase-site.xml on the Phoenix client:"},"5301":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1627","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-configuration","content":"The last two configuration values should match the HBase server-side configuration."},"5302":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1628","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-configuration","content":"As with other configuration properties, phoenix.functions.allowUserDefinedFunctions\nmay be specified at JDBC connection time as a connection property."},"5303":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1629","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-configuration","content":"Example:"},"5304":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1630","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-configuration","content":"The following optional parameter is used by the dynamic class loader to copy JARs from HDFS into the local filesystem:"},"5305":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1631","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-custom-udfs","content":"Implement your custom UDF by following How to write custom\nUDF."},"5306":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1632","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-custom-udfs","content":"Compile your code into a JAR, then deploy the JAR to HDFS. It is recommended\nto add the JAR to the HDFS directory configured by hbase.dynamic.jars.dir."},"5307":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1633","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dropping-the-udfs","content":"You can drop functions using the DROP FUNCTION query.\nDropping a function deletes the metadata for that function from Phoenix."},"5308":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1634","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-write-custom-udf","content":"You can follow these steps to write your UDF (for more detail, see this blog post):"},"5309":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1635","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-write-custom-udf","content":"Create a new class derived from org.apache.phoenix.expression.function.ScalarFunction."},"5310":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1636","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-write-custom-udf","content":"Implement getDataType() to determine the function return type."},"5311":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1637","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-write-custom-udf","content":"Implement evaluate() to calculate the result for each row.\nThe method receives org.apache.phoenix.schema.tuple.Tuple with the current row state and an org.apache.hadoop.hbase.io.ImmutableBytesWritable to populate with the function result.\nThe method returns false if there is not enough information to calculate the result (usually because one argument is unknown), and true otherwise."},"5312":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1638","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-write-custom-udf","content":"Below are additional optimization-related steps."},"5313":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1639","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-write-custom-udf","content":"To contribute to scan start/stop key formation, custom functions need to override the following two methods from ScalarFunction:"},"5314":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1640","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-write-custom-udf","content":"Additionally, to enable ORDER BY optimization or in-place GROUP BY, override:"},"5315":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1641","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-limitations","content":"The JAR containing UDFs must be manually added to and deleted from HDFS. There is ongoing work to add SQL statements for JAR add/remove (PHOENIX-1890)."},"5316":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1642","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-limitations","content":"The dynamic class loader copies UDF JARs to {hbase.local.dir}/jars at the Phoenix client and region server when a UDF is used in queries. These JARs must be deleted manually when a function is deleted."},"5317":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1643","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-limitations","content":"Functional indexes must be rebuilt manually if the function implementation changes (PHOENIX-1907)."},"5318":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1644","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-limitations","content":"Once loaded, a JAR is not unloaded. Use a different JAR for modified implementations to avoid restarting the cluster (PHOENIX-1907)."},"5319":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1645","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-defined-functions-limitations","content":"To list functions, query the SYSTEM.\"FUNCTION\" table (PHOENIX-1921)."},"5320":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1646","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes","content":"Secondary indexes are an orthogonal way to access data from its primary access path. In HBase, you have a single\nindex that is lexicographically sorted on the primary row key. Access to records in any way other than through\nthe primary row requires scanning over potentially all the rows in the table to test them against your filter.\nWith secondary indexing, the columns or expressions you index form an alternate row key to allow point lookups\nand range scans along this new axis."},"5321":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1647","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#covered-indexes","content":"Phoenix is particularly powerful in that we provide covered indexes -\nwe do not need to go back to the primary table once we have found the index entry. Instead, we bundle the data\nwe care about right in the index rows, saving read-time overhead."},"5322":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1648","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#covered-indexes","content":"For example, the following would create an index on the v1 and v2 columns and\ninclude the v3 column in the index as well to prevent having to get it from the data table:"},"5323":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1649","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#functional-indexes","content":"Functional indexes (available in 4.3 and above) allow you to create\nan index not just on columns, but on an arbitrary expressions. Then when a query uses that expression, the index\nmay be used to retrieve the results instead of the data table. For example, you could create an index on UPPER(FIRST_NAME||' '||LAST_NAME)\nto allow you to do case insensitive searches on the combined first name and last name of a person."},"5324":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1650","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#functional-indexes","content":"For example, the following would create this functional index:"},"5325":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1651","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#functional-indexes","content":"With this index in place, when the following query is issued, the index would be used instead of the data table to retrieve the results:"},"5326":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1652","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#functional-indexes","content":"Phoenix supports two types of indexing techniques: global and local indexing.\nEach are useful in different scenarios and have their own failure profiles and performance characteristics."},"5327":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1653","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes","content":"Global indexing targets read heavy uses cases. With global indexes, all the performance penalties for indexes occur at write time. We intercept the data table updates on write (DELETE, UPSERT VALUES and UPSERT SELECT), build the index update and then sent any necessary updates to all interested index tables. At read time, Phoenix will select the index table to use that will produce the fastest query time and directly scan it just like any other HBase table. An index will not be used for a query that references a column that isn't part of the index."},"5328":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1654","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#local-indexes","content":"Local indexing targets write heavy, space constrained use cases. Just like with global indexes, Phoenix will automatically select whether or not to use a local index at query-time. With local indexes, index data and table data co-reside on same server preventing any network overhead during writes. Local indexes can be used even when the query isn't fully covered (i.e. Phoenix automatically retrieve the columns not in the index through point gets against the data table). Unlike global indexes, all local indexes of a table are stored in a single, separate shared table prior to 4.8.0 version. From 4.8.0 onwards we are storing all local index data in the separate shadow column families in the same data table. At read time when the local index is used, every region must be examined for the data as the exact region location of index data cannot be predetermined. Thus some overhead occurs at read-time."},"5329":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1655","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes","content":"A global index is \"covered\" for a query when every column the query references is in\nthe index — either in the index key or in INCLUDE. Historically a global index that\nwasn't covered for a query simply wasn't used: Phoenix would fall back to a full\ndata-table scan. Uncovered indexes lift that restriction — Phoenix can use a\nglobal index even when the query needs columns that aren't in it, by joining back\nto the data table on the server side to fetch the missing columns\n(PHOENIX-6458)."},"5330":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1656","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes","content":"Local indexes already worked this way (see Local Indexes above);\nthe new capability is for global indexes."},"5331":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1657","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Both an INCLUDE covered index and an uncovered index let the same query use the\nindex. They trade off different costs:"},"5332":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1658","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Approach"},"5333":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1659","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Index size"},"5334":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1660","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Read path"},"5335":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1661","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Best when"},"5336":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1662","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"CREATE INDEX ... INCLUDE (c)"},"5337":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1663","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Larger — c is duplicated"},"5338":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1664","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Index-only scan"},"5339":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1665","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Column c is small, frequently read with the index, and updated rarely"},"5340":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1666","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"CREATE UNCOVERED INDEX"},"5341":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1667","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Smaller — only the key"},"5342":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1668","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Index seek + server-side join to data row"},"5343":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1669","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"Column is large, rarely read with this access path, or updated often"},"5344":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1670","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-when","content":"A common pattern is to leave wide payload columns out of the index entirely and\nlet Phoenix fetch them via the join-back when needed. This keeps the index compact\nand write-cheap, at the cost of a per-matching-row data-table read on the rare query\nthat wants the payload."},"5345":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1671","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-usage","content":"Declare an uncovered global index with the UNCOVERED keyword in the DDL.\nUNCOVERED is global-only (cannot be combined with LOCAL) and cannot be combined\nwith INCLUDE — the whole point is to skip duplicating columns:"},"5346":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1672","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#uncovered-indexes-usage","content":"The planner picks an uncovered index automatically when it's the best plan; no\nhint is required. If you want to force it (e.g. to compare plans), use the\nstandard index hint:"},"5347":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1673","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes","content":"A partial index indexes only the rows of the data table that satisfy a SQL\nboolean predicate, supplied at CREATE INDEX time via a WHERE clause. The\nindex is smaller, writes that don't affect the predicate are cheaper, and the\nplanner only uses it for queries whose own predicate implies the index's. Added\nin PHOENIX-7032."},"5348":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1674","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-when","content":"Reach for a partial index when the workload reads a small, well-defined slice of\na much larger table — and that slice is identifiable by a SQL predicate. Common\nshapes:"},"5349":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1675","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-when","content":"Open / active records: WHERE status IN ('OPEN', 'PENDING') on a table that\nalso stores closed records."},"5350":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1676","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-when","content":"Recent rows: WHERE created_at > DATE '2024-01-01'."},"5351":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1677","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-when","content":"Hot tier: WHERE priority >= 5 for paging / alerting workloads."},"5352":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1678","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-when","content":"Failure investigation: WHERE result = 'FAIL' on a successful-most-of-the-time\ntable."},"5353":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1679","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-usage","content":"The DDL is a regular CREATE INDEX with a trailing WHERE clause. Both\ncovered and uncovered global indexes can be partial:"},"5354":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1680","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-usage","content":"The planner uses the index for any query whose WHERE clause implies the\nindex's predicate:"},"5355":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1681","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-mutations","content":"Partial indexes correctly maintain themselves when a mutation moves a row into\nor out of the predicate, including under ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE:"},"5356":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1682","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-mutations","content":"A row that starts satisfying the predicate after an update has its index\nrow created."},"5357":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1683","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-mutations","content":"A row that stops satisfying the predicate after an update has its index\nrow removed."},"5358":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1684","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-mutations","content":"This means a partial index stays consistent for a workload like \"close an\norder\" even though that operation flips the row out of the index's covered\nslice."},"5359":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1685","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-limitations","content":"Local partial indexes are not supported — only global (covered or\nuncovered) partial indexes work today."},"5360":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1686","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#partial-indexes-limitations","content":"The WHERE predicate is fixed at CREATE INDEX time. To change it, drop and\nrecreate the index."},"5361":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1687","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-population","content":"By default, when an index is created, it is populated synchronously during the CREATE INDEX call. This may not be feasible depending on the current size of the data table. As of 4.5, initially population of an index may be done asynchronously by including the ASYNC keyword in the index creation DDL statement:"},"5362":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1688","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-population","content":"The map reduce job that populates the index table must be kicked off separately through the HBase command line like this:"},"5363":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1689","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-population","content":"Only when the map reduce job is complete will the index be activated and start to be used in queries. The job is resilient to the client being exited. The output-path option is used to specify a HDFS directory that is used for writing HFiles to."},"5364":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1690","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-population","content":"You can also start index population for all indexes in BUILDING (\"b\") state with the following HBase command line:"},"5365":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1691","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-index-threshold","content":"As of 4.16 (and 5.1), setting the phoenix.index.async.threshold property to a positive number will disallow synchronous index creation if the estimated indexed data size exceeds phoenix.index.async.threshold (in bytes)."},"5366":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1692","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-usage","content":"Indexes are automatically used by Phoenix to service a query when it's determined more efficient to do so. However, a global index will not be used unless all of the columns referenced in the query are contained in the index. For example, the following query would not use the index, because v2 is referenced in the query but not included in the index:"},"5367":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1693","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-usage","content":"There are two means of getting an index to be used in this case:"},"5368":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1694","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-usage","content":"Create a covered index by including v2 in the index:"},"5369":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1695","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-usage","content":"This will cause the v2 column value to be copied into the index and kept in synch as it changes. This will obviously increase the size of the index."},"5370":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1696","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-usage","content":"Create a local index:"},"5371":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1697","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-usage","content":"Unlike global indexes, local indexes will use an index even when all columns referenced in the query are not contained in the index. This is done by default for local indexes because we know that the table and index data coreside on the same region server thus ensuring the lookup is local."},"5372":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1698","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-removal","content":"To drop an index, you'd issue the following statement:"},"5373":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1699","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-removal","content":"If an indexed column is dropped in the data table, the index will automatically be dropped. In addition, if a covered column is dropped in the data table, it will be automatically dropped from the index as well."},"5374":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1700","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-properties","content":"Just like with the CREATE TABLE statement, the CREATE INDEX statement may pass through properties to apply to the underlying HBase table, including the ability to salt it:"},"5375":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1701","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-properties","content":"Note that if the primary table is salted, then the index is automatically salted in the same way for global indexes. In addition, the MAX_FILESIZE for the index is adjusted down, relative to the size of the primary versus index table. For more on salting see here. With local indexes, on the other hand, specifying SALT_BUCKETS is not allowed."},"5376":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1702","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#consistency-guarantees","content":"On successful return to the client after a commit, all data is guaranteed to be written to all interested indexes and the\nprimary table. In other words, index updates are synchronous with the same strong consistency guarantees provided by HBase."},"5377":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1703","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#consistency-guarantees","content":"However, since indexes are stored in separate tables than the data table, depending on the properties of the table and the\ntype of index, the consistency between your table and index varies in the event that a commit fails due to a server-side\ncrash. This is an important design consideration driven by your requirements and use case."},"5378":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1704","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#consistency-guarantees","content":"Outlined below are the different options with various levels of consistency guarantees."},"5379":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1705","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#local-indexes-1","content":"Since Phoenix 4.8 local indexes are always guaranteed to be consistent."},"5380":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1706","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes-on-transactional-tables","content":"By declaring your table as transactional, you achieve the highest level of consistency guarantee\nbetween your table and index. In this case, your commit of your table mutations and related index updates are atomic\nwith strong ACID guarantees. If the commit fails, then none of your data (table\nor index) is updated, thus ensuring that your table and index are always in sync."},"5381":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1707","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes-on-transactional-tables","content":"Why not just always declare your tables as transactional? This may be fine, especially if your\ntable is declared as immutable, since the transactional overhead is very small in this case. However, if your data\nis mutable, make sure that the overhead associated with the conflict detection that occurs with transactional tables\nand the operational overhead of running the transaction manager is acceptable. Additionally, transactional tables\nwith secondary indexes potentially lowers your availability of being able to write to your data table, as both the\ndata table and its secondary index tables must be availalbe as otherwise the write will fail."},"5382":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1708","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes-on-immutable-tables","content":"For a table in which the data is only written once and never updated in-place, certain optimizations may be made to reduce the write-time overhead for incremental maintenance.\nThis is common with time-series data such as log or event data, where once a row is written, it will never be updated.\nTo take advantage of these optimizations, declare your table as immutable by adding the IMMUTABLE_ROWS=true property to your DDL statement:"},"5383":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1709","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes-on-immutable-tables","content":"All indexes on a table declared with IMMUTABLE_ROWS=true are considered immutable (note that by default, tables are considered mutable).\nFor global immutable indexes, the index is maintained entirely on the client-side with the index table being generated as changes to the data table occur.\nLocal immutable indexes, on the other hand, are maintained on the server-side.\nNote that no safeguards are in-place to enforce that a table declared as immutable doesn't actually mutate data (as that would negate the performance gain achieved).\nIf that was to occur, the index would no longer be in sync with the table."},"5384":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1710","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes-on-immutable-tables","content":"If you have an existing table that you'd like to switch from immutable indexing to mutable indexing, use the ALTER TABLE command as show below:"},"5385":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1711","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes-on-immutable-tables","content":"Global Indexing for Immutable tables has been completely rewritten for version 4.15 (and 5.1)"},"5386":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1712","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#immutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Immutable index updates go through the same three phase writes as mutable index updates do except that deleting or un-verifying existing index rows is not applicable to immutable indexes.\nThis guarantees that the index tables are always in sync with the data tables."},"5387":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1713","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#immutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Indexes on non transactional, immutable tables have no mechanism in place to automatically deal with a commit failure. Maintaining\nconsistency between the table and index is left to the client to handle. Because the updates are idempotent, the simplest\nsolution is for the client to continue retrying the batch of mutations until they succeed."},"5388":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1714","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#global-indexes-on-mutable-tables","content":"Global Indexing for Mutable tables has been completely rewritten for version 4.15 (and 5.1)"},"5389":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1715","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"The new Strongly Consistent Global Indexing feature uses a three-phase indexing algorithm to guarantee that the index tables are always in sync with the data tables."},"5390":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1716","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"The implementation uses a shadow column to track the status of index rows:"},"5391":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1717","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Write:"},"5392":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1718","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Set the status of existing index rows to unverified and write the new index rows with the unverified status"},"5393":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1719","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Write the data table rows"},"5394":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1720","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Delete the existing index rows and set the status of new rows to verified"},"5395":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1721","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Read:"},"5396":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1722","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Read the index rows and check their status"},"5397":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1723","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"The unverified rows are repaired from the data table"},"5398":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1724","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Delete:"},"5399":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1725","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Set the index table rows with the unverified status"},"5400":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1726","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Delete the data table rows"},"5401":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1727","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Delete index table rows"},"5402":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1728","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"See resources for more in-depth information."},"5403":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1729","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"All newly created tables use the new indexing algorithm."},"5404":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1730","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-415-and-51-and-newer-versions","content":"Indexes created with older Phoenix versions will continue to use the old implementation, until upgraded with IndexUpgradeTool"},"5405":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1731","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"For non transactional mutable tables, we maintain index update durability by adding the index updates to the Write-Ahead-Log (WAL) entry of the primary table row.\nOnly after the WAL entry is successfully synced to disk do we attempt to make the index/primary table updates. We write the\nindex updates in parallel by default, leading to very high throughput. If the server crashes while we are writing the index\nupdates, we replay the all the index updates to the index tables in the WAL recovery process and rely on the idempotence of\nthe updates to ensure correctness. Therefore, indexes on non transactional mutable tables are only ever a single batch of\nedits behind the primary table."},"5406":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1732","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"It's important to note several points:"},"5407":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1733","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"For non transactional tables, you could see the index table out of sync with the primary table."},"5408":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1734","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"As noted above, this is ok as we are only a very small bit behind and out of sync for very short periods"},"5409":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1735","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Each data row and its index row(s) are guaranteed to to be written or lost - we never see partial updates as this is part of the atomicity guarantees of HBase."},"5410":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1736","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Data is first written to the table followed by the index tables (the reverse is true if the WAL is disabled)."},"5411":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1737","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Singular Write Path"},"5412":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1738","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"There is a single write path that guarantees the failure properties. All writes to the HRegion get intercepted by our\ncoprocessor. We then build the index updates based on the pending update (or updates, in the case of the batch).\nThese update are then appended to the WAL entry for the original update."},"5413":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1739","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"If we get any failure up to this point, we return the failure to the client and no data is persisted or made visible\nto the client."},"5414":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1740","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Once the WAL is written, we ensure that the index and primary table data will become visible, even in the case of a failure."},"5415":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1741","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"If the server does crash, we then replay the index updates with the usual WAL replay mechanism"},"5416":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1742","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"If the server does not crash, we just insert the index updates to their respective tables."},"5417":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1743","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"If the index updates fail, the various means of maintaining consistency are outlined below."},"5418":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1744","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"If the Phoenix system catalog table cannot be reached when a failure occurs, we force the server to be immediately aborted and failing this, call System.exit on the JVM, forcing the server to die. By killing the server, we ensure that the WAL will be replayed on recovery, replaying the index updates to their appropriate tables. This ensures that a secondary index is not continued to be used when it's in a know, invalid state."},"5419":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1745","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Disallow table writes until mutable index is consistent"},"5420":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1746","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"The highest level of maintaining consistency between your non transactional table and index is to declare that writes to the\ndata table should be temporarily disallowed in the event of a failure to update the index. In this consistency\nmode, the table and index will be held at the timestamp before the failure occurred, with writes to the data\ntable being disallowed until the index is back online and in-sync with the data table. The index will\nremain active and continue to be used by queries as usual."},"5421":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1747","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"The following server-side configurations control this behavior:"},"5422":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1748","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"phoenix.index.failure.block.write must be true to enable a writes to the data table to fail\nin the event of a commit failure until the index can be caught up with the data table."},"5423":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1749","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"phoenix.index.failure.handling.rebuild must be true (the default) to enable a mutable index to\nbe rebuilt in the background in the event of a commit failure."},"5424":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1750","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Disable mutable indexes on write failure until consistency restored"},"5425":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1751","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"The default behavior with mutable indexes is to mark the index as disabled if a write to them fails at commit time,\npartially rebuild them in the background, and then mark them as active again once consistency is restored. In this\nconsistency mode, writes to the data table will not be blocked while the secondary index is being rebuilt. However,\nthe secondary index will not be used by queries while the rebuild is happening."},"5426":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1752","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"The following server-side configurations control this behavior:"},"5427":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1753","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"phoenix.index.failure.handling.rebuild must be true (the default) to enable a mutable index to\nbe rebuilt in the background in the event of a commit failure."},"5428":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1754","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"phoenix.index.failure.handling.rebuild.interval controls the millisecond frequency at which the server\nchecks whether or not a mutable index needs to be partially rebuilt to catch up with updates to the data\ntable. The default is 10000 or 10 seconds."},"5429":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1755","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"phoenix.index.failure.handling.rebuild.overlap.time controls how many milliseconds to go back from the timestamp\nat which the failure occurred to go back when a partial rebuild is performed. The default is 1."},"5430":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1756","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"Disable mutable index on write failure with manual rebuild required"},"5431":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1757","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"This is the lowest level of consistency for mutable secondary indexes. In this case, when a write to a secondary\nindex fails, the index will be marked as disabled with a manual\nrebuild of the index required to enable it to be used\nonce again by queries."},"5432":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1758","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"The following server-side configurations control this behavior:"},"5433":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1759","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mutable-table-indexes-for-414-and-50-and-older-versions","content":"phoenix.index.failure.handling.rebuild must be set to false to disable a mutable index from being\nrebuilt in the background in the event of a commit failure."},"5434":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1760","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulkload-tool-limitation","content":"The BulkLoadTools (e.g. CSVBulkLoadTool and JSONBulkLoadTool) cannot presently generate correct updates to mutable\nsecondary indexes when pre-existing records are being updated. In the normal mutable secondary index write path, we can\nsafely calculate a Delete (for the old record) and a Put (for the new record) for each secondary index while holding a\nrow-lock to prevent concurrent updates. In the context of a MapReduce job, we cannot effectively execute this same logic\nbecause we are specifically doing this \"out of band\" from the HBase RegionServers. As such, while these Tools generate\nHFiles for the index tables with the proper updates for the data being loaded, any previous index records corresponding\nto the same record in the table are not deleted. This net-effect of this limitation is: if you use these Tools to re-ingest\nthe same records to an index table, that index table will have duplicate records in it which will result in incorrect\nquery results from that index table."},"5435":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1761","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulkload-tool-limitation","content":"To perform incremental loads of data using the BulkLoadTools which may update existing records, you must\ndrop and re-create all index tables after the data table is loaded. Re-creating the index with the ASYNC option and\nusing IndexTool to populate and enable that index is likely a must for tables of non-trivial size."},"5436":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1762","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulkload-tool-limitation","content":"To perform incremental loading of CSV datasets that do not require any manual index intervention, the psql tool can\nbe used in place of the BulkLoadTools. Additionally, a MapReduce job could be written to parse CSV/JSON data and write\nit directly to Phoenix; although, such a tool is not currently provided by Phoenix for users."},"5437":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1763","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setup","content":"Non transactional, mutable indexing requires special configuration options on the region server and master to run - Phoenix ensures that they are setup correctly when you enable mutable indexing on the table; if the correct properties are not set, you will not be able to use secondary indexing. After adding these settings to your hbase-site.xml, you'll need to do a rolling restart of your cluster."},"5438":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1764","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setup","content":"As Phoenix matures, it needs less and less manual configuration. For older Phoenix versions you'll need to add the properties listed for that version, as well as the properties listed for the later versions."},"5439":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1765","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-phoenix-412-and-later","content":"You will need to add the following parameters to hbase-site.xml on each region server:"},"5440":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1766","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-phoenix-412-and-later","content":"The above property enables custom WAL edits to be written, ensuring proper writing/replay of the index updates. This codec supports the usual host of WALEdit options, most notably WALEdit compression."},"5441":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1767","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-phoenix-48---411","content":"The following configuration changes are also required to the server-side hbase-site.xml on the master and regions server nodes:"},"5442":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1768","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-phoenix-48---411","content":"The above properties prevent deadlocks from occurring during index maintenance for global indexes (HBase 0.98.4+ and Phoenix 4.3.1+) by ensuring index updates are processed with a higher priority than data updates. It also prevents deadlocks by ensuring metadata rpc calls are processed with a higher priority than data rpc calls."},"5443":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1769","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-phoenix-versions-47-and-below","content":"The following configuration changes are also required to the server-side hbase-site.xml on the master and regions server nodes:"},"5444":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1770","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-phoenix-versions-47-and-below","content":"The above properties are required to use local indexing."},"5445":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1771","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-local-indexes-created-before-480","content":"While upgrading the Phoenix to 4.8.0+ version at server remove above three local indexing related configurations from hbase-site.xml if present. From client we are supporting both online(while initializing the connection from phoenix client of 4.8.0+ versions) and offline(using psql tool) upgrade of local indexes created before 4.8.0. As part of upgrade we recreate the local indexes in ASYNC mode. After upgrade user need to build the indexes using IndexTool"},"5446":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1772","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-local-indexes-created-before-480","content":"Following client side configuration used in the upgrade."},"5447":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1773","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-local-indexes-created-before-480","content":"phoenix.client.localIndexUpgradeThe value of it is true means online upgrade and false means offline upgrade.Default: true"},"5448":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1774","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-local-indexes-created-before-480","content":"Command to run offline upgrade using psql:"},"5449":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1775","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Out the box, indexing is pretty fast. However, to optimize for your particular environment and workload, there are several properties you can tune."},"5450":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1776","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"All the following parameters must be set in hbase-site.xml - they are true for the entire cluster and all index tables, as well as across all regions on the same server (so, for instance, a single server would not write to too many different index tables at once)."},"5451":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1777","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"index.builder.threads.max"},"5452":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1778","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Number of threads to used to build the index update from the primary table update"},"5453":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1779","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Increasing this value overcomes the bottleneck of reading the current row state from the underlying HRegion. Tuning this value too high will just bottleneck at the HRegion as it will not be able to handle too many concurrent scan requests as well as general thread-swapping concerns."},"5454":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1780","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 10"},"5455":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1781","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"index.builder.threads.keepalivetime"},"5456":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1782","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Amount of time in seconds after we expire threads in the builder thread pool."},"5457":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1783","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Unused threads are immediately released after this amount of time and not core threads are retained (though this last is a small concern as tables are expected to sustain a fairly constant write load), but simultaneously allows us to drop threads if we are not seeing the expected load."},"5458":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1784","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 60"},"5459":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1785","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"index.writer.threads.max"},"5460":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1786","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Number of threads to use when writing to the target index tables."},"5461":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1787","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"The first level of parallelization, on a per-table basis - it should roughly correspond to the number of index tables"},"5462":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1788","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 10"},"5463":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1789","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"index.writer.threads.keepalivetime"},"5464":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1790","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Amount of time in seconds after we expire threads in the writer thread pool."},"5465":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1791","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Unused threads are immediately released after this amount of time and not core threads are retained (though this last is a small concern as tables are expected to sustain a fairly constant write load), but simultaneously allows us to drop threads if we are not seeing the expected load."},"5466":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1792","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 60"},"5467":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1793","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"hbase.htable.threads.max"},"5468":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1794","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Number of threads each index HTable can use for writes."},"5469":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1795","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Increasing this allows more concurrent index updates (for instance across batches), leading to high overall throughput."},"5470":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1796","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 2,147,483,647"},"5471":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1797","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"hbase.htable.threads.keepalivetime"},"5472":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1798","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Amount of time in seconds after we expire threads in the HTable's thread pool."},"5473":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1799","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Using the \"direct handoff\" approach, new threads will only be created if it is necessary and will grow unbounded. This could be bad but HTables only create as many Runnables as there are region servers; therefore, it also scales when new region servers are added."},"5474":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1800","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 60"},"5475":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1801","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"index.tablefactory.cache.size"},"5476":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1802","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Number of index HTables we should keep in cache."},"5477":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1803","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Increasing this number ensures that we do not need to recreate an HTable for each attempt to write to an index table. Conversely, you could see memory pressure if this value is set too high."},"5478":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1804","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 10"},"5479":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1805","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"org.apache.phoenix.regionserver.index.priority.min"},"5480":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1806","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Value to specify to bottom (inclusive) of the range in which index priority may lie."},"5481":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1807","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 1000"},"5482":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1808","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"org.apache.phoenix.regionserver.index.priority.max"},"5483":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1809","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Value to specify to top (exclusive) of the range in which index priority may lie."},"5484":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1810","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Higher priorites within the index min/max range do not means updates are processed sooner."},"5485":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1811","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 1050"},"5486":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1812","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"org.apache.phoenix.regionserver.index.handler.count"},"5487":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1813","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Number of threads to use when serving index write requests for global index maintenance."},"5488":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1814","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Though the actual number of threads is dictated by the Max(number of call queues, handler count), where the number of call queues is determined by standard HBase configuration. To further tune the queues, you can adjust the standard rpc queue length parameters (currently, there are no special knobs for the index queues), specifically ipc.server.max.callqueue.length and ipc.server.callqueue.handler.factor. See the HBase Reference Guide for more details."},"5489":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1815","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-tuning","content":"Default: 30"},"5490":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1816","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-performance","content":"We track secondary index performance via our performance framework. This is a generic test of performance based on defaults - your results will vary based on hardware specs as well as you individual configuration."},"5491":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1817","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-performance","content":"That said, we have seen secondary indexing (both immutable and mutable) go as quickly as < 2x the regular write path on a small, (3 node) desktop-based cluster. This is actually pretty reasonable as we have to write to multiple tables as well as build the index update."},"5492":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1818","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"With Phoenix 4.12, there is now a tool to run a MapReduce job to verify that an index table is valid against its data table. The only way to find orphaned rows in either table is to scan over all rows in the table and do a lookup in the other table for the corresponding row. For that reason, the tool can run with either the data or index table as the \"source\" table, and the other as the \"target\" table. The tool writes all invalid rows it finds either to file or to an output table PHOENIX_INDEX_SCRUTINY. An invalid row is a source row that either has no corresponding row in the target table, or has an incorrect value in the target table (i.e. covered column value)."},"5493":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1819","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"The tool has job counters that track its status. VALID_ROW_COUNT, INVALID_ROW_COUNT, BAD_COVERED_COL_VAL_COUNT. Note that invalid rows - bad col val rows = number of orphaned rows. These counters are written to the table PHOENIX_INDEX_SCRUTINY_METADATA, along with other job metadata."},"5494":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1820","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"The Index Scrutiny Tool can be launched via the hbase command (in hbase/bin) as follows:"},"5495":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1821","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"It can also be run from Hadoop using either the phoenix-core or phoenix-server jar as follows:"},"5496":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1822","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"By default two mapreduce jobs are launched, one with the data table as the source table and one with the index table as the source table."},"5497":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1823","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"The following parameters can be used with the Index Scrutiny Tool:"},"5498":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1824","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Parameter"},"5499":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1825","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Description"},"5500":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1826","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-dt,--data-table"},"5501":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1827","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Data table name (mandatory)"},"5502":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1828","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-it,--index-table"},"5503":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1829","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Index table name (mandatory)"},"5504":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1830","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-s,--schema"},"5505":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1831","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Phoenix schema name (optional)"},"5506":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1832","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-src,--source"},"5507":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1833","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"DATA_TABLE_SOURCE, INDEX_TABLE_SOURCE, or BOTH. Defaults to BOTH"},"5508":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1834","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-o,--output"},"5509":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1835","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Whether to output invalid rows. Off by default"},"5510":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1836","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-of,--output-format"},"5511":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1837","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"TABLE or FILE output format. Defaults to TABLE"},"5512":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1838","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-om,--output-max"},"5513":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1839","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Maximum number of invalid rows to output per mapper. Defaults to 1M"},"5514":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1840","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-op,--output-path"},"5515":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1841","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"For FILE output format, the HDFS directory where files are written"},"5516":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1842","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-t,--time"},"5517":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1843","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Timestamp in millis at which to run the scrutiny. This is important so that incoming writes don't throw off the scrutiny. Defaults to current time minus 60 seconds"},"5518":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1844","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"-b,--batch-size"},"5519":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1845","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-scrutiny-tool","content":"Number of rows to compare at a time"},"5520":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1846","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-limitations","content":"If rows are actively being updated or deleted while the scrutiny is running, the tool may give you false positives for inconsistencies (PHOENIX-4277)."},"5521":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1847","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-limitations","content":"Snapshot reads are not supported by the scrutiny tool (PHOENIX-4270)."},"5522":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1848","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"IndexUpgradeTool updates global indexes created by Phoenix 4.14 and earlier (or 5.0) to use the new Strongly Consistent Global Indexes implementation."},"5523":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1849","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"It accepts following parameters:"},"5524":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1850","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"Parameter"},"5525":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1851","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"Description"},"5526":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1852","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"only in version"},"5527":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1853","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-o,--operation"},"5528":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1854","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"upgrade or rollback (mandatory)"},"5529":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1855","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-tb,--tables"},"5530":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1856","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"[table1,table2,table3] (-tb or -f mandatory)"},"5531":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1857","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-f,--file"},"5532":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1858","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"Csv file with above format (-tb or -f mandatory)"},"5533":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1859","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-d,--dry-run"},"5534":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1860","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"If passed this will just output steps that will be executed; like a dry run"},"5535":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1861","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-h,--help"},"5536":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1862","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"Help on how to use the tool"},"5537":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1863","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-lf,--logfile"},"5538":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1864","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"File location to dump the logs"},"5539":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1865","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-sr,--index-sync-rebuild"},"5540":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1866","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"whether or not synchronously rebuild the indexes; default rebuild asynchronous"},"5541":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1867","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"4.15"},"5542":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1868","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-rb,--index-rebuild"},"5543":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1869","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"Rebuild the indexes. Set -tool to pass options to IndexTool"},"5544":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1870","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"4.16+, 5.1+"},"5545":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1871","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"-tool,--index-tool"},"5546":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1872","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"Options to pass to indexTool when rebuilding indexes"},"5547":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1873","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"4.16+, 5.1+"},"5548":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1874","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"For 4.16+/5.1+ either specifying the -rb option, or manually rebuilding the indexes with IndexTool after the upgrade is recommended, otherwise the first access of every index row will trigger an index row repair."},"5549":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1875","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"Depending on whether index is mutable, it will remove Indexer coprocessor from a data table and load new coprocessor IndexRegionObserver. For both immutable and mutable, it will load GlobalIndexChecker coprocessor on Index table. During this process, data table and index table are disabled-loaded/unloaded with coproc-enabled within short time span. At the end, it does an asynchronous index rebuilds. Index reads are not blocked while index-rebuild is still ongoing, however, they may be a bit slower for rows written prior to upgrade."},"5550":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1876","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#index-upgrade-tool","content":"IndexUpgradeTool doesn't make any distinction between view-index and table-index. When a table is passed, it will perform the upgrade-operation on all the 'children' indexes of the given table."},"5551":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1877","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"There have been several presentations given on how secondary indexing works in Phoenix that have a more in-depth look at how indexing works (with pretty pictures!):"},"5552":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1878","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"Slides for Strongly Consistent Global Indexes for Apache Phoenix, 2019 Distributed SQL Summit"},"5553":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1879","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"Recording of Strongly Consistent Global Indexes for Apache Phoenix, 2019 Distributed SQL Summit"},"5554":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1880","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"Slides for Local Secondary Indexes in Apache Phoenix, 2017 PhoenixCon"},"5555":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1881","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"These older resources refer to obsolete implementations in some cases"},"5556":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1882","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"Los Anglees HBase Meetup - Sept, 4th, 2013"},"5557":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1883","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"Local Indexes by Huawei"},"5558":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1884","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"PHOENIX-938 and HBASE-11513 for deadlock prevention during global index maintenance."},"5559":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1885","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-resources","content":"PHOENIX-1112: Atomically rebuild index partially when index update fails"},"5560":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1886","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storage-formats","content":"As part of Phoenix 4.10, we have reduced on-disk storage size to improve overall performance by implementing the following enhancements:"},"5561":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1887","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storage-formats","content":"Introduce a layer of indirection between Phoenix column names and the corresponding HBase column qualifiers."},"5562":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1888","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storage-formats","content":"Support a new encoding scheme for immutable tables that packs all values into a single cell per column family."},"5563":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1889","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storage-formats","content":"For more details on column mapping and immutable data encoding, see this blog."},"5564":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1890","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"You can set the column mapping property only when creating a table. Before deciding to use column mapping, think about how many columns your table and view hierarchy will require over their lifecycle. The following limits apply for each mapping scheme:"},"5565":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1891","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"Config/Property Value"},"5566":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1892","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"Max # of columns"},"5567":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1893","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"1"},"5568":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1894","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"255"},"5569":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1895","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"2"},"5570":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1896","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"65535"},"5571":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1897","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"3"},"5572":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1898","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"16777215"},"5573":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1899","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"4"},"5574":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1900","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"2147483647"},"5575":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1901","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"NONE"},"5576":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1902","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"no limit (theoretically)"},"5577":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1903","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"For mutable tables, this limit applies to columns in all column families. For immutable tables, the limit applies per column family. By default, new Phoenix tables use column mapping. These defaults can be overridden by setting the following config value in hbase-site.xml."},"5578":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1904","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"Table type"},"5579":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1905","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"Default Column mapping"},"5580":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1906","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"Config"},"5581":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1907","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"Mutable/Immutable"},"5582":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1908","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"2 byte qualifiers"},"5583":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1909","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"phoenix.default.column.encoded.bytes.attrib"},"5584":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1910","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-column-mapping","content":"This config controls global defaults that apply to all tables. If you want a different mapping scheme than the global default, use the COLUMN_ENCODED_BYTES table property."},"5585":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1911","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Like column mapping, immutable data encoding can only be set when creating a table. Through performance testing, SINGLE_CELL_ARRAY_WITH_OFFSETS generally provides strong performance and space savings. Below are some scenarios where ONE_CELL_PER_COLUMN encoding may be a better fit."},"5586":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1912","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Data is sparse, i.e. less than 50% of the columns have values."},"5587":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1913","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Size of data within a column family gets too big. With default HBase block size of 64K, if data within a column family grows beyond 50K then SINGLE_CELL_ARRAY_WITH_OFFSETS is generally not recommended."},"5588":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1914","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Immutable tables that are expected to have views on them."},"5589":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1915","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"By default, immutable non-multitenant tables are created using two-byte column mapping and SINGLE_CELL_ARRAY_WITH_OFFSETS data encoding. Immutable multi-tenant tables are created with two-byte column mapping and ONE_CELL_PER_COLUMN data encoding. This is because users often create tenant-specific views on base multi-tenant tables, and as noted above this is more suitable for ONE_CELL_PER_COLUMN. Like column mapping, you can change these global defaults by setting the following configs in hbase-site.xml."},"5590":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1916","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Immutable Table type"},"5591":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1917","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Immutable storage scheme"},"5592":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1918","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Config"},"5593":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1919","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Multi-tenant"},"5594":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1920","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"ONE_CELL_PER_COLUMN"},"5595":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1921","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"phoenix.default.multitenant.immutable.storage.scheme"},"5596":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1922","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"Non multi-tenant"},"5597":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1923","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"SINGLE_CELL_ARRAY_WITH_OFFSETS"},"5598":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1924","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"phoenix.default.immutable.storage.scheme"},"5599":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1925","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"You can also provide specific immutable storage and column mapping schemes with the IMMUTABLE_STORAGE_SCHEME and COLUMN_ENCODED_BYTES table properties. For example:"},"5600":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1926","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"You can choose not to use SINGLE_CELL_ARRAY_WITH_OFFSETS while still using numeric column mapping. For example:"},"5601":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1927","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-use-immutable-data-encoding","content":"When using SINGLE_CELL_ARRAY_WITH_OFFSETS, you must use a numeric column mapping scheme. Attempting to use SINGLE_CELL_ARRAY_WITH_OFFSETS with COLUMN_ENCODED_BYTES = NONE throws an error."},"5602":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1928","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-disable-column-mapping","content":"To disable column mapping across all new tables, set phoenix.default.column.encoded.bytes.attrib to 0. You can also keep it enabled globally and disable it selectively for a table by setting COLUMN_ENCODED_BYTES = 0 in the CREATE TABLE statement."},"5603":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1929","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert","content":"To support atomic upsert, an optional ON DUPLICATE KEY clause, similar to the MySQL syntax, has been\nincorporated into the UPSERT VALUES command as of Phoenix 4.9. The general syntax is described\nhere. This feature provides a superset of the HBase Increment and\nCheckAndPut functionality to enable atomic upserts. On the server-side, when the commit\nis processed, the row being updated will be locked while the current column values are read and the\nON DUPLICATE KEY clause is executed. Given that the row must be locked and read when the ON DUPLICATE KEY\nclause is used, there will be a performance penalty (much like there is for an HBase Put versus a CheckAndPut)."},"5604":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1930","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert","content":"In the presence of the ON DUPLICATE KEY clause, if the row already exists, the VALUES specified will\nbe ignored and instead either:"},"5605":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1931","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert","content":"the row will not be updated if ON DUPLICATE KEY IGNORE is specified or"},"5606":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1932","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert","content":"the row will be updated (under lock) by executing the expressions following the ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE\nclause."},"5607":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1933","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert","content":"Multiple UPSERT statements for the same row in the same commit batch will be processed in the order of their\nexecution. Thus the same result will be produced when auto commit is on or off."},"5608":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1934","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-examples","content":"For example, to atomically increment two counter columns, you would execute the following command:"},"5609":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1935","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-examples","content":"To only update a column if it doesn't yet exist:"},"5610":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1936","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-examples","content":"Note that arbitrarily complex expressions may be used in this new clause:"},"5611":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1937","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"UPDATE_ONLY is a third variant of the ON DUPLICATE KEY clause, available from\nPhoenix 5.3.0 (PHOENIX-7648).\nUnlike UPDATE, it only runs the update-clause expressions when the row already\nexists — it never inserts. If the row is missing, the supplied VALUES are discarded\nand the statement is a no-op. Use it when an UPSERT should be able to update an\nexisting row but must not create a new one."},"5612":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1938","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"If id = 'sku-42' already exists, its qty is decremented under the row lock.\nIf it doesn't, the supplied VALUES are discarded and nothing is written."},"5613":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1939","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"The three forms compared:"},"5614":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1940","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Clause"},"5615":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1941","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Row missing"},"5616":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1942","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Row present"},"5617":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1943","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"ON DUPLICATE KEY IGNORE"},"5618":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1944","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Insert from VALUES"},"5619":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1945","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"No-op"},"5620":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1946","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE ..."},"5621":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1947","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Insert from VALUES"},"5622":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1948","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Run the update"},"5623":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1949","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE_ONLY ..."},"5624":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1950","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"No-op (VALUES discarded)"},"5625":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1951","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-update-only","content":"Run the update"},"5626":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1952","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"A single-row UPSERT or DELETE can append RETURNING * to return the affected row\nas a JDBC ResultSet in the same round-trip, available from Phoenix 5.3.0\n(PHOENIX-7651). The returned\nrow reflects the server-side state after the mutation — including any values\ncomputed by an ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE clause under the row lock."},"5627":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1953","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"This collapses two common patterns into one round-trip:"},"5628":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1954","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"Atomic read-modify-write counters — increment, then read the new value\nwithout a follow-up SELECT (and without risking a different writer slipping in\nbetween)."},"5629":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1955","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"Tombstoning / queue consumers — atomically delete a row and read the payload\nyou just removed, useful for idempotent replay and \"claim the next task\" patterns."},"5630":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1956","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-returning","content":"The statement must affect a single row when RETURNING * is used. Drivers iterate\nthe returned ResultSet exactly like the result of a regular SELECT."},"5631":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1957","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"Java applications using the Phoenix thick JDBC client can drive the same\natomic-return semantics directly through typed methods on PhoenixStatement and\nPhoenixPreparedStatement, without putting RETURNING * in the SQL:"},"5632":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1958","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"executeAtomicUpdateReturnRow — returns the row state after the mutation\n(or the unchanged row when the statement was a no-op). Equivalent to\nRETURNING * in SQL."},"5633":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1959","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"executeAtomicUpdateReturnOldRow — returns the row state before the\nmutation, regardless of whether the mutation was applied. There is no SQL form\nfor this — only the typed API exposes the pre-image, which is the natural fit\nfor audit logs and compare-and-swap-style flows."},"5634":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1960","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-jdbc-thick-client-api","content":"Both return a Pair<Integer, ResultSet> where the integer is 1 if the row was\nmutated and 0 for a no-op, so callers know the outcome without having to inspect\ncell contents. Both require an auto-commit connection."},"5635":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1961","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"The following limitations are enforced for the ON DUPLICATE KEY clause usage:"},"5636":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1962","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"Primary key columns may not be updated, since this would essentially be creating a new row."},"5637":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1963","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"Transactional tables may not use this clause as atomic upserts are already possible through\nexception handling when a conflict occurs."},"5638":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1964","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"Immutable tables may not use this clause as by definition there should be no updates to\nexisting rows."},"5639":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1965","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"The CURRENT_SCN property may not be set on connection when this clause is used as HBase\ndoes not handle atomicity unless the latest value is being updated."},"5640":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1966","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"The same column should not be updated more than once in the same statement."},"5641":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1967","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#atomic-upsert-limitations","content":"No aggregation or references to sequences are allowed within the clause."},"5642":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1968","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping","content":"From v4.8.0 onward, users can map Phoenix schemas to HBase namespaces so that any table created with a schema is created in the corresponding HBase namespace."},"5643":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1969","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping","content":"Earlier, every table (with or without schema) was created in the default namespace."},"5644":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1970","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"Parameters to enable namespace mapping:"},"5645":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1971","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"Property"},"5646":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1972","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"Description"},"5647":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1973","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"Default"},"5648":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1974","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"phoenix.schema.isNamespaceMappingEnabled"},"5649":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1975","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"If enabled, tables created with a schema are mapped to the corresponding namespace. This must be set on both client and server. Once enabled, it should not be rolled back. Older clients will not work after this property is enabled."},"5650":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1976","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"false"},"5651":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1977","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"phoenix.schema.mapSystemTablesToNamespace"},"5652":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1978","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"This takes effect when phoenix.schema.isNamespaceMappingEnabled is also set to true. If enabled, existing SYSTEM tables are automatically migrated to the SYSTEM namespace. If disabled, system tables are created only in the default namespace. This must be set on both client and server."},"5653":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1979","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-configuration","content":"true"},"5654":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1980","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grammar-available","content":"The following DDL statements can be used to interact with schemas:"},"5655":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1981","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grammar-available","content":"CREATE SCHEMA"},"5656":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1982","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grammar-available","content":"USE SCHEMA"},"5657":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1983","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grammar-available","content":"DROP SCHEMA"},"5658":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1984","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-faq","content":"How to migrate existing tables with schema to namespace?"},"5659":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1985","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-faq","content":"How are system tables migrated?"},"5660":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1986","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-faq","content":"What permissions are required to CREATE and DROP SCHEMA?"},"5661":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1987","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-faq","content":"How are schemas mapped for different table types?"},"5662":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1988","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-mapping-faq","content":"What is a namespace and what are the benefits of mapping tables to namespaces?"},"5663":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1989","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-migrate-existing-tables-with-schema-to-namespace","content":"For a Kerberized environment, run with a user that has sufficient permission (admin) to create a namespace."},"5664":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1990","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-migrate-existing-tables-with-schema-to-namespace","content":"A table is mapped only to a namespace with the same name as the schema (schema_name). Currently, migrating an existing table to a different schema or namespace is not supported."},"5665":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1991","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-migrate-existing-tables-with-schema-to-namespace","content":"Usage example:"},"5666":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1992","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url