_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins","content":"09-05-2013"},"6962":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3288","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"Secondary indices will be automatically utilized when running join queries. For example, if we create indices on the \"Orders\" table and the \"Items\" table respectively, which are defined as follows:"},"6963":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3289","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"We can find out each item's total sales value by joining the \"Items\" table and the \"Orders\" table and then grouping the joined result with \"ItemName\" (and also adding some filtering conditions):"},"6964":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3290","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"The results will be like:"},"6965":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3291","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"ItemName"},"6966":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3292","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"OrderValue"},"6967":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3293","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"BX016"},"6968":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3294","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"10374"},"6969":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3295","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"MU3508"},"6970":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3296","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"14400"},"6971":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3297","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"XT2217"},"6972":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3298","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"46436"},"6973":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3299","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"The execution plan for this query (by running EXPLAIN <query>) will be:"},"6974":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3300","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joining-tables-with-indices","content":"In this case, the index table iItems is used in place of the data table Items since the index table iItems is indexed on column ItemName and will hence benefit the GROUP BY clause in this query. Meanwhile, the index table i2Orders is favored over the data table Orders and another index table iOrders because a range scan instead of a full scan can be applied as a result of the WHERE clause."},"6975":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3301","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grouped-joins-and-derived-tables","content":"Phoenix also supports complex join syntax such as grouped joins (or sub joins) and joins with derived-tables. You can group joins by using parenthesis to prioritize certain joins before other joins are executed. You can also replace any one (or more) of your join tables with a subquery (derived table), which could be yet another join query."},"6976":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3302","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grouped-joins-and-derived-tables","content":"For grouped joins, you can write something like:"},"6977":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3303","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grouped-joins-and-derived-tables","content":"By replacing the sub join with a subquery (derived table), we get an equivalent query as:"},"6978":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3304","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#grouped-joins-and-derived-tables","content":"As an alternative to the earlier example where we find each item's sales figures, instead of using GROUP BY after joining two tables, we can join the \"Items\" table with grouped results from the \"Orders\" table:"},"6979":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3305","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hash-join-vs-sort-merge-join","content":"Basic hash join usually outperforms other types of join algorithms, but it has its limitations too, the most significant of which is the assumption that one of the relations must be small enough to fit into memory. Thus Phoenix now has both hash join and sort-merge join implemented to facilitate fast join operations as well as join between two large tables."},"6980":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3306","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hash-join-vs-sort-merge-join","content":"Phoenix currently uses hash join whenever possible since it is usually faster. You can force sort-merge join with hint USE_SORT_MERGE_JOIN. Choosing between algorithms and detecting the smaller relation for hash join is expected to be increasingly guided by table statistics."},"6981":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3307","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"Oftentimes a join will occur from a child table to a parent table, mapping the foreign key of the child table to the primary key of the parent. So instead of doing a full scan on the parent table, Phoenix will drive a skip-scan or a range-scan based on the foreign key values it got from the child table result."},"6982":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3308","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"Phoenix will extract and sort multiple key parts from the join keys so that it can get the most accurate key hints/ranges possible for the parent table scan."},"6983":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3309","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"For example, we have parent table \"Employee\" and child table \"Patent\" defined as:"},"6984":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3310","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"Now we'd like to find employees who filed patents after January 2000 and list their names by patent count:"},"6985":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3311","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"The above statement will do a skip-scan over the \"Employee\" table and will use both join key \"Region\" and \"LocalID\" for runtime key hint calculation. Below is the execution time of this query with and without this optimization on an \"Employee\" table of about 5000000 records and a \"Patent\" table of about 1000 records:"},"6986":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3312","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"W/O Optimization"},"6987":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3313","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"W/ Optimization"},"6988":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3314","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"8.1s"},"6989":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3315","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"0.4s"},"6990":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3316","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#foreign-key-to-primary-key-join-optimization","content":"However, there are times when foreign key values from the child table account for the complete primary key space in the parent table, so skip scans may be slower. You can turn off this optimization with hint NO_CHILD_PARENT_OPTIMIZATION. Table statistics are expected to help make smarter choices between these schemes."},"6991":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3317","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"As mentioned earlier, if we decide to use the hash join approach for our join queries, the prerequisite is that either of the relations can be small enough to fit into memory in order to be broadcast over all servers that have the data of concern from the other relation. And aside from making sure that the region server heap size is big enough to hold the smaller relation, we might also need to pay a attention to a few configuration parameters that are crucial to running hash joins."},"6992":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3318","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"The server-side caches hold the hash table built on the smaller relation. Cache size and lifetime are controlled by the following parameters. A relation may be a physical table, a view, a subquery, or a joined result of other relations in a multi-join query."},"6993":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3319","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"phoenix.query.maxServerCacheBytes"},"6994":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3320","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Maximum size (in bytes) of the raw results of a relation before being compressed and sent over to the region servers."},"6995":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3321","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Attempting to serializing the raw results of a relation with a size bigger than this setting will result in a MaxServerCacheSizeExceededException."},"6996":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3322","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Default: 104,857,600"},"6997":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3323","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"phoenix.query.maxGlobalMemoryPercentage"},"6998":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3324","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Percentage of total heap memory (i.e. Runtime.getRuntime().maxMemory()) that all threads may use."},"6999":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3325","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"The summed size of all living caches must be smaller than this global memory pool size. Otherwise, you would get an InsufficientMemoryException."},"7000":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3326","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Default: 15"},"7001":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3327","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"phoenix.coprocessor.maxServerCacheTimeToLiveMs"},"7002":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3328","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Maximum living time (in milliseconds) of server caches. A cache entry expires after this amount of time has passed since last access."},"7003":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3329","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Consider adjusting this parameter when a server-side IOException (\"Could not find hash cache for joinId\") happens. Getting warnings like \"Earlier hash cache(s) might have expired on servers\" might also be a sign that this number should be increased."},"7004":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3330","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Default: 30,000"},"7005":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3331","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"See our Configuration and Tuning Guide for more details."},"7006":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3332","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-configuration","content":"Although changing parameters can sometimes be a solution to getting rid of the exceptions mentioned above, it is highly recommended that you first consider optimizing the join queries according to the information provided in the following section."},"7007":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3333","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"Now that we know hash joins depend heavily on available memory, instead of immediately changing configuration, it can be enough to understand execution internals and adjust table order in your join query."},"7008":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3334","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"Below is the default join order (without table statistics) and which side of the query is treated as the \"smaller\" relation and put into server cache:"},"7009":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3335","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"lhs INNER JOIN rhsrhs will be built as hash table in server cache."},"7010":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3336","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"lhs LEFT OUTER JOIN rhsrhs will be built as hash table in server cache."},"7011":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3337","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"lhs RIGHT OUTER JOIN rhslhs will be built as hash table in server cache."},"7012":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3338","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"Join order is more complicated with multi-join queries. You can run EXPLAIN <join_query> to inspect the actual execution plan. For multi-inner-join queries, Phoenix applies star-join optimization by default, which means the leading (left-hand-side) table is scanned once while joining all right-hand-side tables at the same time. You can disable this optimization with hint NO_STAR_JOIN if total size of right-hand-side tables exceeds memory limits."},"7013":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3339","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"Let's take the previous query for example:"},"7014":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3340","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"The default join order (using star-join optimization) will be:"},"7015":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3341","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"Alternatively, if we use hint NO_STAR_JOIN:"},"7016":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3342","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"The join order will be:"},"7017":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3343","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimizing-your-query","content":"It is also worth mentioning that not the entire dataset of the table should be counted into the memory consumption. Instead, only those columns used by the query, and of only the records that satisfy the predicates will be built into the server hash table."},"7018":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3344","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-limitations","content":"In our Phoenix 3.3.0 and 4.3.0 releases, joins have the following restrictions and improvements to be made:"},"7019":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3345","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-limitations","content":"PHOENIX-1555: Fallback to many-to-many join if hash join fails due to insufficient memory."},"7020":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3346","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-limitations","content":"PHOENIX-1556: Base hash join versus many-to-many decision on how many guideposts will be traversed for RHS table(s)."},"7021":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3347","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#joins-limitations","content":"Continuous efforts are being made to bring in more performance enhancement for join queries based on table statistics. Please refer to our Roadmap for more information."},"7022":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3348","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries","content":"Phoenix now supports subqueries in the WHERE clause and the FROM clause. Subqueries can be specified in many places, like IN/NOT IN, EXISTS/NOT EXISTS, unmodified comparison operators or ANY/SOME/ALL comparison operators."},"7023":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3349","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-with-in-or-not-in","content":"The following query finds the names of the items that have sales record after Sept 2nd 2013. The inner query returns a list of items that satisfy the search criteria and the outer query will make use of this list to find matching entries."},"7024":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3350","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-with-exists-or-not-exists","content":"EXISTS simply tests the existence of the returned rows by the inner query. If the inner query returns one or more rows, EXISTS returns a value of TRUE; otherwise a value of FALSE. Many EXISTS queries are used to achieve the same goal as with IN queries or with ANY/SOME/ALL comparison queries. The below query returns the same results as the query in the previous example does:"},"7025":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3351","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#semi-joins-and-anti-joins","content":"Queries with IN/NOT IN or EXISTS/NOT EXISTS are implemented with semi-joins and anti-joins wherever possible. A semi-join is different from a conventional join in that rows in the first table will be returned at most once, regardless of how many matches the second table contains for a certain row in the first table. A semi-join returns all those rows from the first table which can find at least one match in the second table. An IN or EXISTS construct is often translated into semi-joins."},"7026":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3352","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#semi-joins-and-anti-joins","content":"An anti-join is the opposite of a semi-join. The results of an anti-join are all those rows from the first table that can find no match in the second table. A NOT IN or NOT EXISTS construct is often translated into anti-joins."},"7027":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3353","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#semi-join-optimization","content":"The \"Foreign Key to Primary Key Join Optimization\" mentioned in Phoenix Joins is equally applied to semi-joins. So if a skip-scan is driven for a semi-join qualified for this optimization and the IN or EXISTS semantics can be fully substituted by the skip-scan alone, the server-side join operation will not happen at all."},"7028":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3354","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-with-comparison-operators","content":"Subqueries can be specified as the right-hand-side operand of the comparison operators (=, <>, >, >=, <, !>, !<, or <=)."},"7029":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3355","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-with-comparison-operators","content":"The example below finds participants whose contest scores are greater than the overall average score."},"7030":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3356","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-with-comparison-operators","content":"A subquery introduced with an unmodified comparison operator (a comparison operator not followed by ANY or ALL) must only return a single row; otherwise it would result in getting a SQL error message."},"7031":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3357","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-with-anysomeall-comparison-operators","content":"Subqueries can be introduced with a comparison operator modified by the keywords ANY, SOME or ALL, which has exactly the same semantics with static arrays, only that the array elements have to be dynamically computed through the execution of the inner query."},"7032":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3358","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-with-anysomeall-comparison-operators","content":"The following query provides an example which lists the orders with a quantity greater than or equal to the maximum order quantity of any item."},"7033":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3359","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#correlated-subqueries","content":"Correlated subqueries (also known as synchronized subqueries) are subqueries that contain references to the outer queries. Unlike independent subqueries, which only need to be evaluated once, the correlated inner query result depends on the outer query values and may differ from row to row."},"7034":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3360","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#correlated-subqueries","content":"The following example finds the patents filed earlier than or equal to all patents filed within the same region:"},"7035":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3361","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#correlated-subqueries","content":"Phoenix optimizes such queries by rewriting them into equivalent join queries so that the inner query only needs to be executed once instead of once per outer row. The correlated subquery above will be rewritten in Phoenix as:"},"7036":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3362","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#correlated-subqueries","content":"Here, collect_distinct() is a reserved internal function in Phoenix, which collects distinct values of a given column or expression into a Phoenix array."},"7037":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3363","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#andor-branches-and-multiple-levels-of-nesting","content":"Correlated subqueries or independent subqueries can be specified anywhere in the WHERE clause, whether in AND branches or in OR branches. And a query can have more than one level of subquery nesting, which means a subquery can include yet another (or more) subquery in itself."},"7038":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3364","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#andor-branches-and-multiple-levels-of-nesting","content":"Below is an example of a complicated query that has multiple levels of subqueries connected with AND and OR branches, which is to find the items not involved in the orders sold to customers in Belgium with a quantity lower than 1000 or to customers in Germany with a quantity lower than 2000:"},"7039":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3365","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#row-subqueries","content":"A subquery can return multiple fields in one row, which is considered returning a row constructor. The row constructor on both sides of the operator (IN/NOT IN, EXISTS/NOT EXISTS or comparison operator) must contain the same number of values, like in the below example:"},"7040":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3366","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#row-subqueries","content":"This query returns all pairs of (column1, column2) that can match any pair of (column3, column4) in the second table after being filtered by condition: column5 = ‘nowhere’."},"7041":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3367","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#derived-tables","content":"Subqueries specified in the FROM clause are also called derived tables. For example, suppose you want to list a set of maximum values of a grouped table by their frequency of occurrence, and the below query will return the desired result:"},"7042":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3368","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#derived-tables","content":"Derived tables can also be specified anywhere in a join query as join-tables. Please refer to the \"Grouped Joins and Derived Tables\" section of Phoenix Joins for more information and examples."},"7043":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3369","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-limitations","content":"In our Phoenix 3.2 and 4.2 releases, the subquery support has the following restrictions:"},"7044":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3370","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-limitations","content":"PHOENIX-1388 Support correlated subqueries in the HAVING clause."},"7045":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3371","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#subqueries-limitations","content":"PHOENIX-1392 Using subqueries as expressions."},"7046":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3372","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"An EXPLAIN plan tells you a lot about how a query will be run:"},"7047":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3373","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"All the HBase range queries that will be executed"},"7048":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3374","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"An estimate of the number of bytes that will be scanned"},"7049":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3375","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"An estimate of the number of rows that will be traversed"},"7050":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3376","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"Time at which the above estimate information was collected"},"7051":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3377","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"Which HBase table will be used for each scan"},"7052":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3378","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"Which operations (sort, merge, scan, limit) are executed on the client versus the server"},"7053":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3379","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"Use an EXPLAIN plan to check how a query will run, and consider rewriting queries to meet the following goals:"},"7054":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3380","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"Emphasize operations on the server rather than the client. Server operations are distributed across the cluster and operate in parallel, while client operations execute within the single client JDBC driver."},"7055":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3381","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"Use RANGE SCAN or SKIP SCAN whenever possible rather than TABLE SCAN."},"7056":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3382","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"Filter against leading columns in the primary key constraint. This assumes you have designed the primary key to lead with frequently-accessed or frequently-filtered columns as described in “Primary Keys,” above."},"7057":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3383","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"If necessary, introduce a local index or a global index that covers your query."},"7058":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3384","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"If you have an index that covers your query but the optimizer is not detecting it, try hinting the query:\nSELECT /*+ INDEX() */ …"},"7059":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3385","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explain-plan-overview","content":"See also: SQL Language Reference - EXPLAIN"},"7060":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3386","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"An explain plan consists of lines of text that describe operations that Phoenix will perform during a query, using the following terms:"},"7061":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3387","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"AGGREGATE INTO ORDERED DISTINCT ROWS — aggregates the returned rows using an operation such as addition. When ORDERED is used, the GROUP BY operation is applied to the leading part of the primary key constraint, which allows the aggregation to be done in place rather than keeping all distinct groups in memory on the server side."},"7062":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3388","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"AGGREGATE INTO SINGLE ROW — aggregates the results into a single row using an aggregate function with no GROUP BY clause. For example, the count() statement returns one row with the total number of rows that match the query."},"7063":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3389","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"CLIENT — the operation will be performed on the client side. It's faster to perform most operations on the server side, so you should consider whether there's a way to rewrite the query to give the server more of the work to do."},"7064":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3390","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"FILTER BY expression—returns only results that match the expression."},"7065":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3391","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"FULL SCAN OVER tableName—the operation will scan every row in the specified table."},"7066":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3392","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"INNER-JOIN — the operation will join multiple tables on rows where the join condition is met."},"7067":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3393","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"MERGE SORT — performs a merge sort on the results."},"7068":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3394","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"RANGE SCAN OVER tableName [ ... ] — The information in the square brackets indicates the start and stop for each primary key that's used in the query."},"7069":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3395","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"ROUND ROBIN — when the query doesn't contain ORDER BY and therefore the rows can be returned in any order, ROUND ROBIN order maximizes parallelization on the client side."},"7070":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3396","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"<x>-CHUNK — describes how many threads will be used for the operation. The maximum parallelism is limited to the number of threads in the thread pool. The minimum parallelization corresponds to the number of regions the table has between the start and stop rows of the scan. The number of chunks will increase with a lower guidepost width, as there is more than one chunk per region."},"7071":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3397","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"PARALLEL <x>-WAY — describes how many parallel scans will be merge sorted during the operation."},"7072":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3398","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"SERIAL — some queries run serially. For example, a single row lookup or a query that filters on the leading part of the primary key and limits the results below a configurable threshold."},"7073":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3399","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"EST_BYTES_READ - provides an estimate of the total number of bytes that will be scanned as part of executing the query."},"7074":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3400","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"EST_ROWS_READ - provides an estimate of the total number of rows that will be scanned as part of executing the query."},"7075":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3401","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#anatomy-of-an-explain-plan","content":"EST_INFO_TS - epoch time in milliseconds at which the estimate information was collected."},"7076":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3402","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#jdbc-explain-plan-api-and-estimates","content":"The information displayed in the explain plan API can also be accessed programmatically through the standard JDBC interfaces. When statistics collection\nis enabled for a table, the explain plan also gives an estimate of number of rows and bytes a query is going to scan. To get hold of the info, you can\nuse corresponding columns in the result set returned by the explain plan statement. When stats collection is not enabled or if for some reason\nPhoenix cannot provide the estimate information, the columns return null. Below is an example:"},"7077":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3403","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"The general process for contributing code to Phoenix works as follows:"},"7078":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3404","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"Discuss your changes on the dev mailing list"},"7079":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3405","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"Create a JIRA issue unless there already is one"},"7080":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3406","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"Setup your development environment"},"7081":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3407","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"Prepare a patch containing your changes"},"7082":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3408","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"Submit the patch"},"7083":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3409","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"These steps are explained in greater detail below."},"7084":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3410","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-process","content":"Note that the instructions below are for the main Phoenix project.\nUse the corresponding repository for the other subprojects.\nTephra and Omid also have their own JIRA projects."},"7085":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3411","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#discuss-on-the-mailing-list","content":"It's often best to discuss a change on the public mailing lists before creating and submitting a patch."},"7086":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3412","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#discuss-on-the-mailing-list","content":"If you're unsure whether certain behavior in Phoenix is a bug, please send a mail to the user mailing list to check."},"7087":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3413","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#discuss-on-the-mailing-list","content":"If you're considering adding major new functionality to Phoenix, it's a good idea to first discuss the idea on the developer mailing list to make sure that your plans are in line with others in the Phoenix community."},"7088":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3414","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#log-a-jira-ticket","content":"The first step is to create a ticket on the Phoenix JIRA."},"7089":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3415","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setup-development-environment","content":"To set up your development environment, see these directions."},"7090":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3416","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#generate-a-patch","content":"There are two general approaches for creating and submitting a patch: GitHub pull requests, or manual patch creation with Git. Both are explained below. Please make sure that the patch applies cleanly on all active branches, including master and the unified 4.x branch."},"7091":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3417","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#generate-a-patch","content":"Regardless of which approach is taken, please make sure to follow the Phoenix code conventions (more information below). Whenever possible, unit tests or integration tests should be included with patches."},"7092":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3418","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#generate-a-patch","content":"Please make sure that the patch contains only one commit, then click the \"Submit patch\" button to automatically trigger tests on the patch."},"7093":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3419","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#generate-a-patch","content":"The commit message should reference the JIRA ticket issue (which has the format\nPHOENIX-{NUMBER}:{JIRA-TITLE})."},"7094":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3420","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#generate-a-patch","content":"To effectively get the patch reviewed, please raise the pull request against an appropriate branch."},"7095":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3421","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#naming-convention-for-the-patch","content":"When generating a patch, make sure the patch name uses the following format:\nPHOENIX-{NUMBER}.{BRANCH-NAME}.{VERSION}.patch"},"7096":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3422","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#naming-convention-for-the-patch","content":"Examples: PHOENIX-4872.master.v1.patch, PHOENIX-4872.master.v2.patch, PHOENIX-4872.4.x-HBase-1.3.v1.patch, etc."},"7097":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3423","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#github-workflow","content":"Create a pull request in GitHub for the mirror of the Phoenix Git repository."},"7098":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3424","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#github-workflow","content":"Generate a patch and attach it to JIRA so Hadoop QA runs automated tests."},"7099":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3425","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#github-workflow","content":"If you update the PR, generate a new patch with a different name so patch changes are detected and tests run for the new patch."},"7100":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3426","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#local-git-workflow","content":"Create a local branch:"},"7101":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3427","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#local-git-workflow","content":"Make and commit changes"},"7102":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3428","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#local-git-workflow","content":"Generate a patch based on the JIRA issue number:"},"7103":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3429","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#local-git-workflow","content":"Attach the created patch file to the JIRA ticket."},"7104":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3430","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#code-conventions","content":"The Phoenix code conventions are similar to the Sun/Oracle Java Code Convention. We use 4 spaces (no tabs) for indentation and limit lines to 100 characters."},"7105":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3431","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#code-conventions","content":"Eclipse code formatting settings and import order settings (which can also be imported into Intellij IDEA) are available in the dev directory of the Phoenix codebase."},"7106":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3432","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#code-conventions","content":"All new source files should include the Apache license header."},"7107":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3433","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#committer-workflow","content":"In general, the \"rebase\" workflow should be used with the Phoenix codebase (see this blog post for more information on the difference between \"merge\" and \"rebase\" workflows in Git)."},"7108":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3434","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#committer-workflow","content":"A patch file can be downloaded from a GitHub pull request by adding .patch to the end of the pull request URL, for example: https://github.com/apache/phoenix/pull/35.patch."},"7109":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3435","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#committer-workflow","content":"When applying a user-contributed patch, use git am when a fully formatted patch file is available, as this preserves contributor contact information. Otherwise, add the contributor's name to the commit message."},"7110":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3436","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#committer-workflow","content":"If a single ticket consists of a patch with multiple commits, the commits can be squashed into a single commit using git rebase."},"7111":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3437","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Review the How to Contribute documentation."},"7112":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3438","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Sign up for a GitHub account if you do not have one."},"7113":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3439","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Go to the Phoenix GitHub repository and create a fork, which creates {username}/phoenix."},"7114":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3440","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Set up Git locally."},"7115":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3441","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Instructions"},"7116":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3442","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Download"},"7117":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3443","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Make sure you have a JDK (Phoenix historically required JDK 7)."},"7118":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3444","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Make sure you have Maven 3+ installed."},"7119":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3445","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#develop-getting-started","content":"Add the following to your 